window.harnessChunks["rows-2f7ffaf56a74f99c.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T20:55:47Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pydantic AI Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai-harness/tree/434649f87d34aa7e5d48c7bd26ad33c50d57cf10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 434649f87d34aa7e5d48c7bd26ad33c50d57cf10\">434649f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute a bounded coding/research task using first-party Harness tools, workspace/context capabilities and model-driven action selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository/web/task ambiguity, file and shell state, tool results, test failures, retrieved evidence, long-horizon context pressure and intermediate errors encountered while completing the task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive investigation/tool/edit/research action and iterate from resulting observations until the assigned task is completed or otherwise concluded. - Decision owner: the model-driven Pydantic AI Agent configured and exported through the Harness&#39;s first-party Coder/Researcher path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Coder/Researcher combined capabilities, filesystem/shell/web tools, repository context, context compaction/output limits, memory, guardrails/spend controls and the Pydantic AI Agent execution substrate. - Closure path: user task → packaged Harness-enabled Agent → model-selected tool/actions → file/shell/web/tool observations → further model decisions → result/changes/evidence. - Boundary reachability: the repository ships a runnable pydanticaiharness.coder:coderagent and documents zero-setup CLI execution; users may also instantiate Agent(..., capabilities=[Coder()]) directly. The assessed actor is therefore wired into a supported first-party Harness mode rather than existing only in examples/dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: first-party capabilities constrain and expose actions, but the autonomous model decides which actions to take from current operational evidence; deterministic tool/runtime machinery does not preselect the task solution. - Evidence: README.md, pydanticaiharness/coder/agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Pydantic AI core provides the generic Agent loop as a dependency; this assessment does not import unrelated core metasystem capabilities. S1 credit rests on this repository&#39;s shipped concrete Harness agent/capability composition and documented autonomous operating path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently evidenced S2-specific inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation loop is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete cross-S1 interference witness meeting the Profile threshold is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an S2-specific attenuation relation. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubAgents and DynamicWorkflow provide named delegation, isolation, fan-out, chaining, voting, retry control and result transport; budgets bound child execution. - Closure path: no qualifying inter-S1 conflict/oscillation → S2-specific attenuation → returned change in later S1 behaviour loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model can coordinate in the ordinary software sense, but the Methodology requires more than routing/delegation/sequencing/shared results. Parallel alternatives, critic scoring and workflow dependencies do not by themselves prove that distinct S1 units are interfering and that a separate coordination relation attenuates that interference. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/dynamicworkflow/README.md, README.md. - Basis: explicit absence after function-first review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a downstream workflow can define a real S2 disturbance and relation, but general DynamicWorkflow expressiveness is not enough for a repository-level positive S2 claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: in DynamicWorkflow mode, regulate the current whole-team portfolio of subordinate agent calls, dependencies, retries and result-conditioned follow-up commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing intermediate subagent results, failed review outcomes, alternative candidate quality, fan-out resource needs and dependencies that require deciding which child work to launch, retry, chain, select or stop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the workflow program that allocates child-agent calls and conditionally changes subsequent team commitments based on current returned results. - Decision owner: the parent orchestration agent model, which writes the runworkflow Python program from the current task and available subagent catalog. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DynamicWorkflow capability, Monty sandbox, named-agent catalog, isolated child Agent.run calls, asyncio fan-out, result values, hard maxagentcalls and usage/resource limits. - Closure path: current task + available team catalog → parent model writes workflow → child S1 runs return live results → model-authored program branches/selects/retries/dispatches later children → final team output returns to parent operation. - Boundary reachability: DynamicWorkflow is a shipped documented Harness capability. Supplying a catalog of named Pydantic AI agents gives the parent model the standard runworkflow tool; no downstream scheduler/control-plane implementation is required for the autonomous parent to write and execute current-control logic. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the sandbox and budgets deterministically execute/enforce the generated program, but the discretionary whole-team choreography and conditional intervention logic is authored by the parent model for the current task. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/dynamicworkflow/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this positive S3 applies to the supported DynamicWorkflow team mode, not every single-agent Coder run. Preconfigured hard budgets and sandbox resource limits are supporting enforcement, not the ownership basis. - Whole-system current view: the generated workflow program holds the set of team members it can call and receives intermediate results from all child runs used in that workflow, allowing later calls/selection/retry to depend on current team outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: allocate/fan out child calls, sequence dependencies, compare/select candidate outputs, retry failed/reviewed work and launch follow-up agents from current intermediate evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review the live operational trajectory with a second model and return corrective steering into the running production agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: instruction drift, unsupported claims, scope creep, hallucination/security concerns and other run-level deviations that may not be detected by the production agent&#39;s ordinary self-report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge the bounded recent trajectory as AllGood or produce a specific Steer(message) correction. - Decision owner: the separate model-driven TrajectoryJudge agent/model instance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cadence/window hooks, bounded transcript rendering, concurrent judge evaluation, enforced verdict output type, RunContext.enqueue(..., &#39;asap&#39;), attribution and run usage/failure accounting. - Closure path: live production trajectory → separate judge receives raw recent transcript/tool evidence → independent AllGood/Steer verdict → Steer enqueued to production conversation → next model request receives correction → subsequent production behaviour changes. - Boundary reachability: TrajectoryJudge is a shipped documented capability that can be added directly to an Agent with its own model/instructions or a full distinct judge Agent; the steering enqueue path is supplied by the capability itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment is made by a second autonomous model rather than by deterministic validation or the same production actor; the runtime only schedules the review and transports the verdict. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/trajectoryjudge/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the judge sees a rendered recent trajectory rather than independently executing the environment itself; the complementary path is direct access to user/assistant/tool-call/tool-result evidence rather than relying on the production agent&#39;s final summary. - Claim being audited: the live agent run remains on-task, evidence-grounded and otherwise compliant with the judge&#39;s specified concern. - Ordinary reporting path: the production agent&#39;s own messages, final output and normal task/tool loop. - Complementary access path: the judge receives a bounded raw transcript including user messages, assistant messages, tool calls and tool results on an independent cadence while the run is still active. - Independence boundary: a separate model or full Agent instance owns its own instructions/model configuration and cannot be replaced by the production agent&#39;s self-evaluation without removing the credited judge actor. - Who acts on findings: the Harness capability enqueues Steer into the running production conversation, and the production agent receives it on the next model request and changes subsequent action accordingly.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: expose a first-party path by which an agent can detect a missing host behavior during operational work, generate and validate a new reusable capability, and persist that adaptation for future activation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task/environment requirements reveal that the current host lacks a needed guardrail, instruction, tool or lifecycle hook that would improve future operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what new capability to author and generate its Python implementation from the observed need; activation of that authored capability in later operation still requires host/orchestrator composition. - Decision owner: the running agent owns adaptation-option generation; the complete future-capability closure is not autonomous because the surrounding orchestrator/application must load and pass active authored capabilities into later runs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: authorcapability, import/construction/static-getter validation, persistent source files + manifest.json, listing/disabling and store.loadactive(). - Closure path: current task exposes missing host behavior → agent authors/validates/persists capability → active capability store records option → documented host integration loads active capability into a subsequent agent.run → future capability changes. The final activation edge requires composition, which is why state is C rather than A. - Boundary reachability: CapabilityCreation is a shipped Harness capability with default agent-facing guidance and persistence/validation machinery. The S4-specific option-generation path is first-party; only the documented next-run activation loop remains for the developer/orchestrator to compose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: option discovery/authorship is agent-owned, but the library explicitly states that capabilities cannot enter the live run and that the orchestrator owns the integration contract for loading them on subsequent runs; therefore autonomous S4 closure is incomplete. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/capabilitycreation/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream application that implements the documented load-active loop may close autonomous S4 for that separate composed system-in-focus, but that composition is not credited back to this repository assessment. - External distinction: during a coding/operational task the agent discovers that the host lacks a needed behavior such as a guardrail, tool, instruction or hook. - Future / prospective distinction: authored capabilities cannot affect the already-running execution; they are explicitly intended for activation on the next run or a later loop iteration. - Adaptation option generated: a validated persistent AbstractCapability implementation authored by the agent and recorded in the capability store/manifest. - Path back into current capability / S3: store.loadactive() returns persisted active capabilities for the host to pass into a later agent.run; this is a function-specific return path but requires the host/orchestrator integration edge to close.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity / ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the reviewed agent-organization recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy disturbance established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system instructions, guardrails, spend/tool limits, tool approval, permission/configuration and capability composition constrain behaviour but do not constitute ultimate-policy ownership. - Closure path: no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance of subsequent organization loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-owned task planning/workflow control and self-extension remain operational/current-control/adaptation functions. They do not show the agent deciding the organization&#39;s ultimate identity/policy, and user-authored constraints do not become S5 merely because they are authoritative configuration. - Evidence: README.md, pydanticaiharness/capabilitycreation/README.md. - Basis: explicit absence after boundary review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: tool approval and operator configuration can represent legitimate human authority over particular actions without establishing a complete parent S5 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9828806eeec61c022133c2a70e42890196b90300/assessments/pydantic-ai-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9828806eeec61c022133c2a70e42890196b90300\">9828806 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T02:45:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SafeAI-Lab-X/ClawKeeper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ClawKeeper</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SafeAI-Lab-X/ClawKeeper/tree/69db077317bd8a89777c7670295539ed93145f46\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 69db077317bd8a89777c7670295539ed93145f46\">69db077 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: make a contextual safety decision for a proposed tool action and close that decision into the host execution boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: benign versus malicious or drifted shell/filesystem/network/tool actions, prompt-injection consequences, credential access/exfiltration chains, contextual false positives and bypasses that require intent/history-sensitive judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose allow, ask, or deny for the proposed action after interpreting current intent, recent trajectory and deterministic evidence. - Decision owner: the model-backed Watcher role in the enabled Watcher mode; deterministic guards/post-filter constrain and can override that decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic guard chain; Watcher prompt; post-filter; session history; host adapter; pre-tool hook; HTTP Watcher client. - Closure path: host proposes tool call → deterministic findings + intent/history reach Watcher → model-backed safety proposal → deterministic post-filter → adapter returns block/allow result → host skips or proceeds with the tool operation. - Boundary reachability: the Watcher daemon/client and host adapter are shipped first-party integration surfaces; downstream users configure a model endpoint but do not need to invent the safety role or enforcement feedback path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the Watcher model, deterministic guards still enforce known patterns but cannot make the same contextual judgment over intent and recent trajectory. The model therefore owns the open-ended safety discretion in this first-party mode. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/agent.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/tools.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/policy.py; clawkeepercore/adapters/hermes.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: host-agent task reasoning is not credited. S1 is ClawKeeper&#39;s own safety operation, not the external coding operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material same-recursion inter-S1 coordination function was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple guard modules and external tool calls coexist, but they are stages/inputs in one safety path rather than distinct autonomous S1 units whose interaction produces an evidenced interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S2. - Decision owner: not established as a first-party S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordered guard chains; deterministic strictest-wins post-filter; per-session history; adapter routing. - Closure path: guard findings are combined inside one safety decision path; no separate inter-S1 conflict→coordination→changed S1-behavior loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: aggregation, routing and arbitration among guard outputs do not by themselves establish S2. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/policy.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/tools.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: coordination among external agents protected by one deployment would belong to that host organization unless independently closed by ClawKeeper.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current protected agent trajectory as a whole and intervene in the next tool action when its relation to intent/history or current risk becomes unsafe. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multi-step drift, attack chains that become suspicious only across recent calls, mismatch between stated intent and proposed action, current deterministic findings and accumulated session context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: at each proposed tool call, use session-level intent and recent-call state to permit the current trajectory to continue, require escalation, or halt the action before execution. - Decision owner: the model-backed Watcher role, subject to stricter deterministic post-filter vetoes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded per-session history; remembered intent; deterministic findings; Watcher prompt; fail-safe; post-filter; blocking pre-tool hook. - Closure path: current session intent + recent tool history + proposed next action → Watcher whole-session safety judgment → deny/ask/allow → host pre-tool enforcement changes the next operation → the call is appended to session history for the following current-control decision. - Boundary reachability: Watcher history, evaluator, client and Hermes pretoolcall enforcement are shipped and wired as a supported optional ClawKeeper mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic rules can veto known hardline cases, but contextual current-trajectory intervention depends on model judgment over the whole recent session. Removing the model removes that discretionary supervisory choice. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/agent.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/tools.py; clawkeepercore/adapters/hermes.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is safety current-control over the protected session, not task prioritization for the external host agent. - Whole-system current view: stated intent, bounded recent tool-call trajectory, proposed next call and current deterministic guard findings for the protected session. - Current-control decision scope: whether the session may execute the proposed next tool action, must ask/escalate, or must block it now.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: on-demand audit and scanners provide materially different evidence, but no standard first-party feedback path automatically or authoritatively closes those findings back into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: configuration weaknesses, dangerous skills, historical log patterns and control-catalogue findings can be detected outside the routine pre-tool path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as a closed S3 audit judgment that necessarily returns into subsequent current regulation. - Decision owner: audit findings are deterministic; remediation requires a separate operator/client invocation of maintenance or manual action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /v1/audit; log/skill scanners; finding scores; harden; rollback; next-step recommendations. - Closure path: audit/scanner reads independent state/history → returns findings/recommendations; any transition into harden or manual remediation is a separate external action, so the required findings→control closure is not first-party-complete. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the evidence path is complementary, but observability plus an optional separate remediation API is insufficient to credit a closed S3 function. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/audit.py; clawkeepercore/scanner.py; clawkeepercore/maintenance.py; clawkeepercore/server.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host/operator composition can close this loop; that external composition is not credited to ClawKeeper alone.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: learn a new future-facing deterministic guard from an observed external attack/bypass pattern and return it into live safety capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel malicious commands or close variants that evade the existing deterministic guard repertoire but are recognized contextually by the Watcher. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generalize a confidently detected bypass into a candidate regex/guard target and decide whether it is specific enough to become a persistent future guard. - Decision owner: the model-backed Watcher synthesis role generates the adaptation; deterministic validation rejects invalid, duplicate or benign-overmatching candidates before activation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: high-confidence missed-guard trigger; synthesis prompt; benign corpus; regex compilation; LearnedPatternStore; guard-target classification; hot-reload bridge; optional upstream fingerprint sharing. - Closure path: external proposed call bypasses deterministic guards → Watcher denies/asks with high confidence → model synthesizes a generalized pattern → validation/persistence accepts it → applylearnedpatterns() mutates live guard rules → future calls are regulated by the new capability. - Boundary reachability: learning, persistence and hot reload are called directly by the shipped Watcher evaluator when the trigger condition holds; no downstream glue is required beyond enabling Watcher mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic memory is not credited; the positive witness is the model&#39;s prospective generalization of a newly observed threat into a reusable guard option that is automatically returned to present capability. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/agent.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/learner.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/reload.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: optional upstream sharing is not needed for the local S4 closure; the credited loop is local learning from externally presented attack variety. - External distinction: an action that passed all current deterministic guards but is judged dangerous by the contextual Watcher. - Future / prospective distinction: the synthesized regex is explicitly required to match the observed command and close variants, so it is a guard for future related actions rather than a record of the single episode. - Adaptation option generated: a model-generated candidate pattern plus target guard class and confidence, bounded by benign-corpus and regex validation. - Path back into current capability / S3: accepted candidates are persisted and immediately hot-reloaded into live deterministic guard modules used by subsequent pre-tool safety decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: safety policy, confirmation thresholds, guard rules and fail-safe behavior constrain execution but remain ordinary operational safety policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue. - Decision owner: configuration/operator for policy values; Watcher and deterministic guards apply those values within current safety operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: default policy; deterministic hardline vetoes; config; guard rules; operator confirmation path. - Closure path: not applicable at S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a safety filter or policy file does not become S5 merely because it has final veto power over ordinary tool calls. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/judge.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/policy.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a parent organization may treat some security policy as identity-level governance; that higher-recursion authority is not a first-party ClawKeeper function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a070822fa4ce9b9dc6fda1c62a7c3cb6a37697e2/assessments/clawkeeper.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a070822fa4ce9b9dc6fda1c62a7c3cb6a37697e2\">a070822 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T13:04:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SethGammon/Citadel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Citadel</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SethGammon/Citadel/tree/38f3a4e7fa13cd48cfbedde872778b847af6c842\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 38f3a4e7fa13cd48cfbedde872778b847af6c842\">38f3a4e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute coding, research, review or test tasks in the target repository through first-party scoped agent roles and skills. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable user requirements, actual repository state, tool errors and test results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured coding model chooses and revises substantive code/research/tool steps to fulfill each bounded task. - Decision owner: provider-backed agent operating bundled Citadel skills/prompts; provider internal inference and generic vendor tools are external. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /do routing, installed skill definitions, delegate assignments, Git worktree/checkpoint state, evidence and callbacks. - Closure path: user outcome → route to model-backed specialist or delegate → tool/source/test work → returned result and observed evidence → correction and delivery. - Boundary reachability: the declared pinned /do and /archon instructions are shipped plugin skills; model executor is separately installed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model, scripts can route/validate but do not solve arbitrary engineering work. - Evidence: skills/do/SKILL.md; skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/fleet/SKILL.md; skills/review/SKILL.md - Basis: direct installed skill invocation and source code -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent inter-agent interference by detecting overlapping writable scopes and holding a competing work claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous operational agents editing the same module/worktree or conflicting merge ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: allow a first-party scope claim only when findOverlap returns no existing colliding writable scope, otherwise throw SCOPEOVERLAP. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic coordination constructor; assignment choices still belong to model orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: claim registry, prefix-overlap checks, read-only exemptions, task/worktree isolation and workflow claim/release instructions. - Closure path: agents choose bounded scopes → claimScope compares active write owners → overlap aborts competing claim/feedback → orchestrator must re-scope/serialize work; release restores opportunity. - Boundary reachability: core claims module is source-reachable via project-local coordination tooling and shipped /archon/fleet steps rather than merely in a document. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: conflict resolution is a deterministic constructor, not independently model-owned S2, and no global lock guarantee follows. - Evidence: core/coordination/claims.js; core/coordination/instances.js; skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/fleet/SKILL.md - Basis: source-structural explicit collision mechanism - Confidence: medium, with non-atomic cross-process race caveat - Caveats: the lookup/write sequence has a TOCTOU race; this is bounded mitigation not complete exclusion. - Distinct S1 units: two or more concurrent model-backed fleet/campaign delegates with declared writable project paths. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing work on overlapping source paths can overwrite or invalidate sibling changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: scope-overlap comparison and refusal before writing the new ownership claim; optional isolated worktrees. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: on SCOPEOVERLAP, competing work is held/re-scoped instead of legitimately continuing under the claimed scope. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: checks a material competing-write relation between operational agents and returns a refusal rather than just recording messages or tasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current allocation, accountability, intervention and recovery across the scoped Citadel campaign&#39;s operational tasks and delegates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled phases, failed verifications, scope collisions, cost/resource constraints and changing evidence about current delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model Archon decides campaign phase order, delegate assignments, direction corrections, retries and escalation based on whole-campaign ledger and evidence. - Decision owner: model-backed Archon current controller; deterministic scripts/checkpoints/evidence tests are enforcement or support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: campaign/feature/decision logs, context handoff, scope-claims, phase validator returns, command/checkpoint/human gates. - Closure path: campaign state and active phase results → Archon model chooses intervention/redelegate/hold/advance → updates campaign and delegate action → subsequent verifier/status feedback. - Boundary reachability: explicit supported /archon skills instruct complete campaign read, phase choice, delegation, corrective and completion decisions, not just scheduling. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the Archon model is conceptually removed, the state/gates cannot select revised goals, allocate priorities or choose rework among alternatives. - Evidence: skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/do/SKILL.md; core/coordination/claims.js; agents/phase-validator.md - Basis: first-party model-led shipped campaign mode - Confidence: medium-high for source-described model authority - Caveats: no separate parent-governed S3 mode credited merely because user can approve a blocking step. - Whole-system current view: active campaign, phase end conditions, feature/decision ledger, delegation/claim context, blockers and verification evidence. - Current-control decision scope: choose work owners/phase resources, rework, interruption, corrections, failure escalation and delivery closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: complementary independently judged audit of operational producer claims using direct reality evidence rather than re-parsing ordinary handoffs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer HANDOFF claims may be inconsistent with changed code or actual behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinct direct-artifact reviewer could return findings to compel new producer work, but entire function-specific independently owned path is not proven. - Decision owner: unresolved: phase-validator checks handoff claims, read-only arch-reviewer can examine files and /review independently reads diff; their binding integration into campaign rework is not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated reviewer persona, five-pass review skill, phase-end evidence runner and phase validator. - Closure path: plausible independent reviewer real file/diff inspection → finding → Archon action/rework; inspected default requirement instead validates HANDOFF plus ordinary test gates. - Boundary reachability: all reviewer artifacts and /archon are packaged at exact source, yet distinct reviewer invocation and audit-specific correction path are not proven in a supported completed mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: read-only role designation or test gate is insufficient to declare a separate audit authority. - Evidence: agents/arch-reviewer.md; skills/review/SKILL.md; skills/archon/SKILL.md; core/verification/profiles.js - Basis: plausible in-boundary pathway without decisive independent closure - Confidence: medium confidence in explicit uncertainty - Caveats: do not infer mandatory external reviewer, S3 autonomy, or independent quality from phase validator alone. - Claim being audited: a phase&#39;s implementation and evidence really meet architectural, code/test and requested constraints. - Ordinary reporting path: delegate HANDOFF/phase completion and verification status to Archon. - Complementary access path: separate read-only reviewer can inspect actual source and changes; not proven tied to every qualifying phase. - Independence boundary: dedicated read-only reviewer is distinct as a persona but first-party review-to-rework call path cannot yet be established. - Who acts on findings: Archon can re-delegate remediation in general, but whether findings from a distinct audit alter phase advancement is unproven.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: explicit prospective adaptation of skill capabilities to future task/environment requirements using measured feedback and model-led exploration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new/failing performance criteria, changed repository constraints, skill drift, unfamiliar target scores and recurring usability shortcomings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in opt-in /evolve mode, model director generates hypotheses/options, chooses improvement attack axes, applies tested nonregressing skill edits and cross-pollinates confirmed patterns. - Decision owner: configured model director running shipped /evolve skill; operator sets target and approved rubric, exceptional rubric &#39;level-up&#39; returns to parent but no distinct closed parent S4 mode claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoring/rubrics, scouting agents, isolated worktree experiments, belief model, evidence logs, verification oracle, commit/reload-skills and /learn pattern compilation. - Closure path: observed external/task scores → prospective hypothesis → independent scout → model selects future skill edit → tested modification/commit → reload or future session uses changed skill, with repeated reassessment. - Boundary reachability: shipped /evolve is user-invocable and explicitly names Phase 5 modification + Phase 6 evidence compilation + Phase 7 cross-skill application; requires approved rubric and configured provider. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model autonomously selects and applies evidence-backed future skill changes within its declared constraints; a static wiki or generator alone would not do this. - Evidence: skills/evolve/SKILL.md; skills/learn/SKILL.md; skills/do/SKILL.md - Basis: explicit skill-defined source-reachable prospective action loop - Confidence: medium; no witnessed trials or measured improvement - Caveats: this is conditional opt-in skill adaptation rather than default project control or automatically evolving the external model; rubric choice remains human prior policy. - External distinction: skill/target quality feedback from tasks and evaluation inputs plus actual test/regression results, not simply next action on current task. - Future / prospective distinction: updates stored skill behavior for future independent tasks and sibling skills, with pattern library cross-pollination. - Adaptation option generated: hypothesis-driven skill patch, verified axis change or transferable pattern from observed scores. - Path back into current capability / S3: verified edits to SKILL.md are committed and reloaded where supported or applied in the next model session, changing future task routing/behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct ultimate organizational ethos/identity settlement path proven for the local project Citadel campaign. - Disturbance / variety regulated: fundamental project/fleet identity and normative tensions between current operations and adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimate ultimate policy right and normative return to mission operations would be required; no separate material path identified. - Decision owner: human user chooses installation, project policy and approves risk/level-up; the model is not ultimate identity authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project-local configuration, hook permissions, risk governance, rubrics and controlled user decisions. - Closure path: permissions, checkpoints and human approval govern task/skill changes but do not constitute independent first-party whole-system ultimate-policy closure. - Boundary reachability: inspected /do/archon/evolve policy, project configuration, risk/manual gates and role prompts at pinned ref; no external organization borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary approval of risky actions and metric rubric edits is not S5, and no first-party S5 autonomous loop found. - Evidence: skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/evolve/SKILL.md; core/config/identity.js; skills/do/SKILL.md; docs/OPERATIONCONTROL.md - Basis: bounded negative at local project operation boundary -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/48c335b7bb4ff17d555163ebb83764b8d1a42a37/assessments/citadel.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 48c335b7bb4ff17d555163ebb83764b8d1a42a37\">48c335b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T23:19:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/scion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Scion (Google Cloud)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/scion/tree/bdf5b6d135bb7585e242d2f08f4c54b3de2c2940\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bdf5b6d135bb7585e242d2f08f4c54b3de2c2940\">bdf5b6d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded project work through an autonomous LLM/harness loop inside a Scion-managed identity, workspace and tool environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous tasks, repository/workspace state, tool results, model observations, command failures and domain-specific uncertainty encountered while producing the assigned outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-local reasoning, actions/tools and follow-up behavior needed to complete the assigned work within the selected harness and template constraints. - Decision owner: the model-driven runtime agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scion container/runtime provisioning, templates, harness-config resolution, credentials/secrets, workspace mounts, project identity, platform skills, limits and Hub/Runtime-Broker lifecycle plumbing. - Closure path: user/orchestrator assigns a task → Scion provisions the agent → the model/harness chooses actions and consumes observations in its own loop → produced artifact/result returns to the user/orchestrator/project. - Boundary reachability: scion start and the documented local/Hub modes provision supported harness agents directly; platform skills and workspace context are injected during standard provisioning rather than requiring repository-development wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scion controls execution substrate and boundaries, while the substantive task-local reasoning/action choices belong to the model-driven agent process. - Evidence: README.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/reference/agent-config.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: underlying harness/model software is external. The ownership claim concerns the autonomous agent Scion provisions and governs through its first-party runtime boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate destructive workspace/filesystem interference among distinct concurrently operating project agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sibling agents working against the same project can collide through shared working trees, branches, files, state or credentials, producing conflicting edits or cross-contamination. - Decisive decision or feedback right: place agent executions into a workspace-sharing regime that isolates mutable work per agent and carry later Git reconciliation through the isolated branch/worktree context. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic/project-configured workspace provisioning; no autonomous S2 actor is established for the decisive isolation choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktree-per-agent and clone-per-agent sharing modes, per-agent branches, dedicated home/state, shadow mounts, project-scoped identity and the injected git-sandbox conflict-resolution protocol. - Closure path: multiple S1 agents are admitted to one project → Scion provisions separate worktrees/clones/branches → subsequent edits occur on isolated work surfaces rather than one mutable checkout → later merge/rebase reconciliation resolves any remaining Git conflict before integration. - Boundary reachability: worktree-per-agent is documented for local Git projects and clone-per-agent for Hub-managed Git projects; the provisioning code creates the per-agent worktree/branch and git-sandbox is conditionally injected for Git workspaces in the shipped runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2-specific attenuation relation is first-party and operational, but workspace isolation is selected/enforced by project/runtime policy rather than an autonomous agent deciding how to coordinate competing S1 units. The agent-side Git skill supports reconciliation but does not transfer ownership of the isolation decision. - Evidence: docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/local/workspace.md; pkg/agent/provision.go; resources/platformskills/git-sandbox/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: shared-plain intentionally permits a non-isolated mode, so the credited S2 path is the supported Git-isolation modes rather than a claim that every Scion project always receives this attenuation. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently running project agents, each with its own identity and task outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent access to one repository/workspace can create overlapping edits, branch/worktree collisions or cross-contamination of mutable project state. - Attenuating coordination relation: worktree-per-agent/clone-per-agent provisioning separates working state and branch context for each S1 agent; shadow/per-agent state isolation further prevents sibling access to agent-local state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each agent&#39;s later file and Git operations are constrained to its provisioned worktree/clone/branch; integration occurs only through a later merge/rebase step rather than uncontrolled shared writes. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Scion explicitly ties the workspace-sharing modes and isolation machinery to preventing interference/conflicts between concurrent agents, rather than merely moving messages or assigning tasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current project/team portfolio of agents and commitments by inspecting live agent state and intervening in worker lifecycle, recovery, timing and cleanup. - Disturbance / variety regulated: completed, blocked, stalled, crashed or limits-exceeded agents; unanswered human questions; concurrent-agent load; failed starts; stuck sessions; workers whose artifacts are not yet safely preserved; and timed work that must survive individual worker sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which workers to create, inspect, continue, interrupt, recover, recreate, stop or delete; verify that owed artifacts exist before cleanup; and route recurring/timed work through a long-lived orchestrator. - Decision owner: the model-driven team orchestrator/creator agent in the standard agent-management path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scion list, scion look, logs, ancestry-based descendant management rights, lifecycle state machine, messaging, scheduler, Runtime Broker and Hub state persistence. - Closure path: current project/worker state becomes visible to the orchestrator → the agent diagnoses current need/state → it issues create/message/recovery/stop/delete/schedule actions → Scion enforces the transition → the changed project portfolio is visible for the next control decision. - Boundary reachability: platform skills are injected by Scion provisioning; team-creation defines an orchestrator that creates/manages workers; scion-agent-manage exposes project-agent inspection/lifecycle operations; scion-scheduler explicitly directs timed agent creation through a long-lived orchestrator so lifecycle remains owned by a reasoning actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Hub/runtime deterministically records and executes lifecycle transitions, but the choice among recovery, continuation, recreation, cleanup and new work is made by the autonomous orchestrator from current project state. - Evidence: resources/platformskills/team-creation/SKILL.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/SKILL.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/references/troubleshooting.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/references/agent-lifecycle.md; resources/platformskills/scion-scheduler/SKILL.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deletion of a project initiator/lead is intentionally reserved to explicit human instruction. That is a bounded parent constraint on agent S3 authority, not by itself a separately evidenced complete parent-governed S3 mode. - Whole-system current view: within the declared project/team recursion, the orchestrator can list the active project agents, inspect individual phase/activity/detail and logs, receive child state-change notifications and inspect project-scoped schedules/history. - Current-control decision scope: worker admission/creation, lifecycle and recovery, current task-agent continuity, cleanup/resource release, timed callbacks/recurring work and management of the orchestrator&#39;s descendant hierarchy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no standard-distribution complementary audit function is established at the declared product boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as a first-party S3 loop; ordinary worker self-report or producer output can be inspected/reviewed, but Scion does not itself wire a sufficiently independent auditor with a distinct access path and corrective control return. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: logs, telemetry, scion look, arbitrary reviewer/security-auditor templates and team-creation review-cycle composition can support an audit organization but do not themselves supply its independence or closure. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Boundary reachability: no qualifying built-in reviewer/auditor path was found in the injected platform skills or documented standard team/runtime contract. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user can construct a reviewer role, and Scion&#39;s own repository contains development-time independent reviews, but generic role/template creation plus same-repository dogfood does not establish an S3-specific product path. - Evidence: resources/platformskills/team-creation/SKILL.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this does not claim Scion cannot host an excellent independent reviewer; it means the reviewed standard distribution does not itself close or specifically construct that organizational independence. - Claim being audited: not fixed by the standard distribution. - Ordinary reporting path: workers return artifacts/results and expose normal state/logs to their creator/orchestrator. - Complementary access path: not established as a built-in independent path; custom reviewer templates can be created but their access/independence is adopter-defined. - Independence boundary: not established in the standard operating contract. - Who acts on findings: not established by a standard S3 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: redesign the agent organization&#39;s future role/workflow/capability structure in response to new or changed project/user requirements. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a task/domain may require new specialist roles, changed collaboration topology, a different sequential/parallel/debate/review workflow, new reusable skills or a restructuring of an existing team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: infer required roles and workflow from a high-level description, choose the orchestrator/workers and communication pattern, and create or modify the templates/skills that determine later team behavior. - Decision owner: the autonomous agent using the shipped team-creation platform skill. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: .scion/templates/ format, scion-agent.yaml, role instructions/system prompts, local or registry skill references, default-template inheritance and standard scion start --type execution. - Closure path: a new external/project requirement is presented → the agent inspects existing roles/workflow when applicable → it generates adaptation options and writes revised/new team templates → a subsequent orchestrator/worker run uses those templates → current organizational capability changes. - Boundary reachability: team-creation is embedded in resources/platformskills/ and platform skills are injected during normal agent provisioning; the resulting templates are the documented first-party mechanism for starting specialized agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user may initiate the request and bound the objective, but the skill explicitly gives the model agent the analysis/design work of identifying roles, workflow and orchestration and turning that judgment into executable team configuration. - Evidence: resources/platformskills/team-creation/SKILL.md; pkg/agent/provision.go; docs-site/src/content/docs/reference/agent-config.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is an agent-owned, explicitly invoked organizational adaptation path, not evidence of unsolicited/background self-evolution. A user can also author templates manually; that parallel constructor path does not negate the autonomous mode. - External distinction: the skill begins from the user&#39;s/project&#39;s changed high-level operating requirement and existing team state rather than only internal chain-of-thought or backlog ordering. - Future / prospective distinction: the generated or modified templates define roles, workflow and capabilities for subsequent agent/team runs. - Adaptation option generated: add/modify a role, alter worker/orchestrator instructions, choose a sequential/parallel/debate/review workflow, change communication flow or package/reference a new skill. - Path back into current capability / S3: the generated templates are directly startable by Scion; the orchestrator then creates/manages workers under the changed role/workflow contract, making the adaptation operative in later current-control and S1 behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure path is established for the Scion project/team at the chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: system prompts, templates, RBAC, secret scopes, project settings and lifecycle authority constrain identity/access/operation, but no reviewed standard path turns a contested organizational-purpose or ultimate-policy issue into a legitimate final decision that is returned to govern the team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S5 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: template/system-prompt text, project settings, profiles, access controls, ancestry scopes, user permissions and human-reserved deletion of leads/initiators. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Boundary reachability: no qualifying agent-owned or parent-governed identity/ultimate-policy loop was found in the standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: editable prompts/config and human authority over ordinary lifecycle actions do not become S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy issue, legitimate decision authority and a return path into subsequent operation. - Evidence: docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/reference/agent-config.md; resources/platformskills/team-creation/SKILL.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/references/agent-lifecycle.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the user remains the practical owner of many project-level choices, but generic user authority/configuration is not sufficient evidence for P. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: not established by the reviewed standard runtime. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: no S5 mode claimed. - Return-to-operation path: no identity/ultimate-policy closure path established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/46449a99c5ee41d135550b2068688dcf962bcc37/assessments/scion-gcp.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 46449a99c5ee41d135550b2068688dcf962bcc37\">46449a9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T18:07:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/theam/limina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Limina</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/theam/limina/tree/56a4e2f3c9c613c7bc61f6a260ade379b21cd782\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 56a4e2f3c9c613c7bc61f6a260ade379b21cd782\">56a4e2f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform the research transformation by selecting hypotheses, experimental approaches, evidence collection, analysis and findings in the mission environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain mechanisms, failed or conflicting experiments, variable baselines and unanticipated research obstacles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select a concrete hypothesis/experiment, interpret results and decide the next research action. - Decision owner: autonomous Codex/Claude project research agent in managed mode, or the user-started agent in the installed template. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: H/E/F validation, canonical artifact IDs, workspace, engine continuation, database transactions, guardrails, mission limits and local templates. - Closure path: model-selected commands create/complete H/E/F and return evidence into the next research checkpoint/ACTIVE plan; the next model turn consumes durable state. - Boundary reachability: default managed runtime calls the first-party research service with a project-scoped capability; template AGENTS instructions and kb scripts operate from the generated project. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model decides substantive research hypotheses, procedure and interpretation; the runtime enforces transaction requirements but cannot select these research judgments with comparable discretion on its own. - Evidence: agent mission prompt and research tools, hypothesis and experiment creation, finding publication after evidence, template research loop. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the objective and limits originate with the parent; H/E/F file creation alone is not an autonomous judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: damp destructive concurrent experiment writes among separate research work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two experiment lanes attempting to claim or mutate the same E artifact, stale versions overwriting newer evidence, or competing top-level strategic decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant or reject a per-experiment claim, isolate concurrent writes, and return lease conflicts to the agent lane for reallocation. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party database lease/CAS mechanism makes the immediate claim decision; a first-party specific coordination feedback path is supplied, but no separate autonomous S2-level conflict-resolution policy/agent is required or shown by the standard distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-experiment work leases, per-project coordinator lease, per-kind atomic ID counters, append-only observations, compare-and-swap decisions and scoped agent lane identity. - Closure path: rejected conflicting claims prevent the competing lane from writing; the caller receives a conflict and can select another E, wait or hand off. Successful separate experiment claims proceed concurrently. - Boundary reachability: managed runtime documents SDK subagent lanes and exposes the authenticated experiment.claim and experiment completion path to model/tool calls in the shipped server. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: lease adjudication is configured deterministic machinery, not an independently evidenced discretionary coordination agent; the provider may decide what to do after a conflict, but agent-owned S2 arbitration across lanes is not established as an operating requirement. - Distinct S1 units: independently operating experiment/subagent lanes, each conducting a bounded experiment and producing own observations. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate claims and non-serializable edits to the same running experiment or stale findings. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive E-scoped leases, versioned completion and separate leases for distinct E artifacts. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a failed claim/invalid version is returned by first-party research commands; only the lease owner may complete its experiment, enabling retry/reallocation without clobbering observations. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the claim/lease is keyed to a demonstrated concrete concurrent evidence-writing conflict, not just to the order of tool calls or dispatch. - Evidence: architecture coordination matrix and subagent lanes, claim-experiment lease, completion owner check, lease conflict and CAS implementation. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a single-agent template project need not instantiate parallel S1 units; C is credited from the supported managed multi-lane constructor, not generic provider subagent capability.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate a research project&#39;s active commitments, objectives, running/waiting state and practical allocation of current attention under a coherent mission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel experimental work diverging from the current priority, resource or provider failures, stalled decisions, unsatisfied access prerequisites, and operator reprioritization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive whole-project objective/step and whether research is RUNNING, WAITING, or COMPLETE; in parent mode, interrupt, reprioritize, pause or resume whole-project work. - Decision owner: base mode — project research model returns structured currentobjective/nextstep/status; parent mode — authenticated human OWNER/EDITOR reviews current state and supplies binding runtime lifecycle or high-priority guidance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordinator lease, versioned checkpoint, database/continuation store, retry backoff, runtime capability, human inbox and CLI/REST/MCP transport. - Closure path: agent checkpoint persists and becomes the next prompt/current research direction. Authenticated user intervention is sequenced into the durable inbox and delivered live or on wake; pause/stop interrupts and changes subsequent runtime execution. - Boundary reachability: all paths occur in the normal managed project supervisor and public project-operations layer, not dogfood-only governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic retries and hard lifecycle controls are support/parent authority; the model owns the discretionary research-priority checkpoint and cannot be replaced by the lease service without losing the same judgment. - Whole-system current view: project mission, ACTIVE snapshot, current coordinator/inbox, resource state, findings and project review status. - Current-control decision scope: present experiments, current objective, next step, blocker, active-turn interruption and commitment to completion/waiting. - Evidence: snapshot and model-owned structured checkpoint, provider-independent decision schema, authenticated steering/lifecycle operations, inbox return. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for base; medium-high for separately owned parent mode. - Caveats: automatic failure retry alone would not qualify as autonomous S3.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: complementary, sufficiently independent access to operative evidence that can challenge routine experiment/reporting claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential self-reporting or measurement overstatement in a research finding. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent auditor with a separately governed corrective feedback path is established in the shipped managed or template mode. - Decision owner: not established; the producing research agent or ordinary human viewer can re-read/review evidence, but no independent audit ownership is wired. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event telemetry, evidence revisions, review endpoints, validation scripts and optional devil&#39;s-advocate CR note templates. - Closure path: evidence review and challenge can change an agent&#39;s ordinary direction, but source does not require a materially independent complementary audit actor feeding the control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the research-devil-advocate instruction asks the same model/session to criticise its current approach; this is reflective production judgment rather than an independent S3 path. The public review endpoint only exposes existing H/E/F state. - Evidence: review endpoint returns project status and records, devil&#39;s-advocate same-agent review skill, runtime feedback and telemetry. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: separately composed human/agent auditors could use exported raw data, but are not automatically shipped and closed as a first-party independent mode here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: investigate external research mechanisms and future options, challenge current strategic ceilings, and revise the project research capability/direction in dialogue with current work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing state of the art, unforeseen external constraints, invalid assumptions and future inadequacy of the present approach. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select an externally informed alternative mechanism/strategy and decide to continue, narrow, reset or pivot after comparing it with current evidence. - Decision owner: autonomous research agent in the installed template mode exercises the research-direction decision via external SOTA search and CR/SR; authenticated parent operator can separately steer future direction after viewing H/E/F evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installable exploratory-sota-research and research-devil-advocate skills, L/CR/SR notes, mission/ACTIVE links, managed guidance and stored findings. - Closure path: external literature/challenge evidence generates prospective alternative H/SR; model commits revised direction into ACTIVE/next research objective and selects the next E. In parent mode, operator strategy decision enters durable guidance and is incorporated into the next model checkpoint, changing future work. - Boundary reachability: shipped template&#39;s AGENTS.md routes explicit research and strategic review through bundled skills when installed by first-party installskills workflow; managed first-party runtime also accepts outside inputs and persistent steering without borrowing repo-development dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model generates and judges candidate future directions, not just stores an external event or summarizes literature. A human can initiate a distinct supervisory future-direction decision through the supported review/steer path. - External distinction: landscape of mechanisms, external benchmark/reproducibility claims, risk and alternative approaches rather than only the current local task list. - Future / prospective distinction: whether a research strategy will generalize or plateau under future conditions, which mechanism family could replace it. - Adaptation option generated: CR/SR recommendation to pivot, reset, narrow or redesign experiments. - Path back into current capability / S3: revise kb/ACTIVE.md/currentobjective and subsequent H/E/F plan based on the external option and parent/agent decision. - Evidence: skill routing and strategic review flow, external SOTA candidate research to persisted artifacts, devil&#39;s-advocate pivot and ACTIVE update, strategic review direction template, human steer delivery. - Basis: explicit supported template mode, structural feedback. - Confidence: medium; scope is adaptation of the mission&#39;s research strategy, not proof that Limina autonomously rewrites its own product runtime. - Caveats: ordinary hypothesis selection alone is S1 rather than S4; this state is supported by the separate external/prospective research-and-pivot route. Some template skills require the documented installation step and are not silently assumed to be installed by every managed deployment.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain ultimate authority for the mission&#39;s identity, success criteria, risk boundaries and unresolved policy choices at parent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a research outcome or new option conflicts with the human-defined mission, permitted resources, stopping conditions or evidence trust threshold. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether mission purpose, non-negotiable boundaries or blocked-stop authority should change; this is deliberately not delegated to autonomous research. - Decision owner: parent human creating and governing the project, not the Codex/Claude model and not the database validator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mission CHALLENGE.md, project success criteria, auth roles, escalation/steering inbox, user review, session recovery contract and runtime limits. - Closure path: first-party template instructs the agent to stop and ask for mission/strategic decisions; parent decides and changes authoritative challenge/boundaries or supplies durable guidance; on recovery/next prompt the agent rereads the challenge/accepted input and operates under it. In managed mode, original project identity is stored by user at kickoff and editable only in CREATED state; general steer is not mistaken for a guaranteed post-start database mission rewrite. - Boundary reachability: the template installation asks the operator for objective/boundaries/stopping conditions, runs in the generated project, and AGENTS.md instructs asking for mission decisions and reconstructing identity from CHALLENGE. This parent loop exists in documented first-party template mode without borrowing developer governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may surface a policy choice but cannot legitimately redefine the user&#39;s research mission; system configuration and stored text enforce/transport a decision rather than owning ultimate authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: change permitted research scope, success criteria, ethical/resource boundary, or stop condition when discovery invalidates the original agreement. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: authenticated project owner / legitimate human sponsor in the template/managed project, no first-party autonomous S5 claimant. - Return-to-operation path: explicit question/blocker → parent decision → updated authoritative mission/guardrail or accepted guidance → model re-grounding and subsequent plan. - Evidence: first-party install skill collects mission and blocked stop, AGENTS mission authority, ask and recovery, agent prompt asks for mission decisions, project identity stored at creation, draft identity fields locked post-start. - Basis: explicit parent-governed project/template contract and structural closure. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: one-time mission setup or generic approval alone would not be enough; the credited parent mode includes escalation and returned identity-level decision. No automatic project-wide self-governance is claimed.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/be5e1da133ae8dc79a8121872500367da9295e69/assessments/li/limina.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit be5e1da133ae8dc79a8121872500367da9295e69\">be5e1da &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T20:47:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ntorga/agent-starter-kit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Starter Kit</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ntorga/agent-starter-kit/tree/851e942ad94f98819cfe550da99c033444c10397\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 851e942ad94f98819cfe550da99c033444c10397\">851e942 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce scoped software implementation, design, context and review outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code details, task failure, architecture facts and acceptance criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose local implementation/investigation steps, execute native host tools, verify and return a handoff. - Decision owner: Registered model-backed framework personas, including Coder. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Supported host inference/tools, dispatch envelope, plan/todo/memory files and native/CLI transport. - Closure path: Maestro dispatches a persona identity plus bounded task → persona performs/validates work → structured handoff → correction or delivery. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party persona specifies the autonomous action/feedback procedure; host machinery executes it. Coder chooses how to satisfy the brief while yielding scope/commit authority. - Evidence: README.md; ENTRYPOINT.md; personas/maestro.md; skills/dispatch/SKILL.md; personas/coder.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Static source evidence; no live model/provider run or empirical viability claim. - Boundary reachability: README installation and ENTRYPOINT boot wire the registered personas to explicit native and CLI dispatch instructions. No bespoke persona workflow is required after installation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate concrete shared-file interference among parallel coding personas. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent dispatches with overlapping file scopes can overwrite/conflict; too many concurrent jobs exceed the prescribed team concurrency. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Compare scopes, choose non-overlapping parallel groups and serialize unavoidable overlaps. - Decision owner: Maestro agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dispatch envelopes, plan parallelizable-group annotations and host task/CLI execution. - Closure path: Scope declarations and plan/handoffs inform Maestro grouping; serialized or narrowed dispatch changes which persona can write which files next. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent chooses the grouping from actual scopes; the static limit and transport are supporting constraints, not ownership. - Evidence: skills/dispatch/SKILL.md; skills/plan-management/SKILL.md; personas/maestro.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Instruction-level scope regulation; no universal filesystem lock/compliance guarantee. - Boundary reachability: Maestro is required to load dispatch and plan-management; dispatch guardrails explicitly forbid overlap and cap simultaneous subagents at three. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more dispatched coders/personas performing independently scoped outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping parallel file writes and excessive concurrent dispatch load. - Attenuating coordination relation: Do not overlap file scopes; serialize when unavoidable; at most three parallel subagents. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Plans/handoffs identify current/next scopes; subsequent dispatches are regrouped, sequenced or narrowed. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The shipped rule directly addresses file-write collisions between units; dispatch is only its carrier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the installed team&#39;s current model resources, delivery commitments and exceptional intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Low-confidence/low-quality provider output, task difficulty and new facts contradicting the agreed feature plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base: choose/upgrade model tier, switch failed provider and redispatch/correct current work. Parent: decide changes to settled feature scope/commitments after escalation. - Decision owner: Base: Maestro. Parent: user who owns the project&#39;s agreed requirements and scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider/model registry, scoped dispatch, persistent plan/session state, Architect grounding and review gates. - Closure path: Base: handoff/review evidence → agent resource/provider/intervention choice → new dispatch. Parent: contradictory fact or changed scope → Maestro presents issue → user resolves → revised/re-grounded plan is reviewed → later Coder epic uses the returned decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model/resource and corrective commitment judgments are agent-owned in the base mode; settled-scope revision remains with the legitimate local user in the parent mode. Host CLI invocation and saved plans enforce/transport those choices. - Evidence: personas/maestro.md; skills/dispatch/SKILL.md; skills/plan-management/SKILL.md; skills/architect-impl-grounding/SKILL.md; skills/agent-memory/SKILL.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Parent control is at the local feature/team recursion; ordinary git commit approval alone is not its S3 witness. - Boundary reachability: Maestro dispatch escalation, dispatch tier/fallback rules and plan-management grounding/revision are mandatory parts of full orchestration; both modes are first-party. - Whole-system current view: Maestro receives persona handoffs, epic progress, review verdicts and current plan/session state across the installed team. - Current-control decision scope: Current provider/tier resources, replacement/corrective dispatch and user-reserved changes to the feature&#39;s settled commitments.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Complement reviewer truth/completeness reports with direct sampled operational evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Reviewers can hallucinate blockers or falsely report no findings while missing risky changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect raw code to confirm reported findings and sample omitted error/auth/data-mutation/I/O paths, then correct reviewer assumptions, provider or framework instructions. - Decision owner: The review-loop assigns this audit-like judgment to Maestro, but autonomous authority is not fully established because Maestro&#39;s broad red line forbids direct scanning/hands-on work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer handoffs, changed-file list and direct code access through the host; verdict/redispatch state. - Closure path: The supplied procedure confirms/discards claims, samples missing findings and feeds diagnosed context/design/model limitations into changed briefs/framework/provider and redispatch; authority conflict must be resolved to claim autonomous closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A specific complementary judgment/feedback path is supplied, but composition must reconcile the designated actor&#39;s conflicting authority before A can be credited. Ordinary per-epic/final QA is not itself the positive witness. - Evidence: skills/review-loop/SKILL.md; personas/maestro.md; personas/reviewer.md; skills/dispatch/SKILL.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium; function-specific constructor established, autonomous authority unresolved - Caveats: The positive is the separate checking of reviewers&#39; claims, not mandatory QA merely renamed as audit. No independent review was executed in this assessment. - Boundary reachability: Maestro explicitly invokes skills/review-loop/SKILL.md; step 5 exposes the function-specific raw evidence and returned control procedure in the shipped full mode. This is a concrete constructor, not arbitrary reviewer extensibility. - Claim being audited: Reviewer blocker validity and completeness, including suspiciously clean verdicts on complex changes. - Ordinary reporting path: Reviewer pass/partial-pass/fail handoff from routine code/plan reviews. - Complementary access path: Maestro spot-checks claims against actual code, then samples the first three high-risk paths for missing findings. - Independence boundary: Different role from Coder and reporting Reviewer; direct raw code can challenge their reports. Designated Maestro authority conflicts with its no-scanning red line, so fully closed autonomous independence/ownership is not asserted. - Who acts on findings: Maestro is specified to discard false positives and alter context/instructions/provider or redispatch; a composed authority resolution is still needed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop for this installed team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider changes, reviewer errors and accumulated observations may motivate improvement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Switching a failing current provider, repairing ambiguous briefs, storing lessons or pulling an updated framework addresses present execution/internal history; no prospective external-option loop is established. - Evidence: skills/boot/SKILL.md; skills/agent-memory/SKILL.md; skills/review-loop/SKILL.md; skills/plan-management/SKILL.md; personas/maestro.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy issue and authoritative return loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User intent, safety/commit rules and plan contradictions limit local work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Plan scope, acceptance and provider choices are current control; static red lines, risk confirmations and memory preferences are not ultimate-identity closure. - Evidence: personas/maestro.md; skills/agent-decision/SKILL.md; skills/agent-memory/SKILL.md; skills/plan-management/SKILL.md; rules/README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e72d8122bea991d98f228af63739c589468a14f0/assessments/ag/agent-starter-kit.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e72d8122bea991d98f228af63739c589468a14f0\">e72d812 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T16:02:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/majiayu000/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rust Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/majiayu000/harness/tree/7f75dee2cb2eaf7195c9b8938ac76db691f1d157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7f75dee2cb2eaf7195c9b8938ac76db691f1d157\">7f75dee &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce coding changes or analyses by running environment-coupled external-model-backed coding agents under the first-party harness lifecycle. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Novel issue requirements, source/test failures, changing repository files and live agent/tool return results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured coding model selects relevant next edits/tools and incorporates results; first-party adapter/session/process creates the executable environment and returns output/evidence. - Decision owner: Project-registered coding-agent runtime actor instantiated through supported Claude/Codex/OpenCode adapters. External provider supplies inference service; the agent task operates within Harness&#39; managed execution boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harness exec, tool/permission scope, agent spawn supervisor, sandbox/proxy, workflow runtime job, attempt/lease record and result. - Closure path: User issue/instruction → agent adapter launch → model-chosen action/tool → repository/file observation → subsequent agent step → recorded task artifact/PR outcome. - Boundary reachability: Shipped CLI quickstart runs actual coding agent, and fleet dispatcher enqueues registered runtime activity to the source adapter; absent provider access would block execution rather than silently replace agent choice with a fake internal model. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Source code supplies boundaries and enforces permissions; the model-backed coding actor chooses context-dependent edits, unlike a deterministic prewritten macro. - Evidence: codexadapter.rs:235-330, spawnsupervisor.rs:1-135, dispatcher.rs:139-335, README quickstart. - Basis: Explicit model/agent/task return path in actual supported deployment. - Confidence: High source-structural, no hosted provider execution run for this assessment. - Caveats: Provider tool auth/sandbox are prerequisites; static policy denial does not independently perform coding work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate resource contention/priority crowding between separately running agent workflows sharing a constrained runtime-profile daily budget. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent claimable work under different runtime profiles can exhaust shared profile-specific budget bands or starve lower-usage profiles, creating unfair cross-agent dispatch competition. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configurable policy chooses runtime profile limits and dailythrottleratio; first-party dispatcher senses usage and an alternative claimable profile and then defers the over-threshold profile&#39;s command in Enforce mode. - Decision owner: The operator owns threshold/prioritization policy; first-party dispatcher implements a function-specific composition contract, not a model-owned discretionary negotiation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RuntimeProfileSelector, shared usage accounting, peekclaimablecommands, throttle-band detector, dispatch barrier/defer backoff and shadow/event auditing. - Closure path: Multiple independently executing agent workflows → competing runtime-profile budget spend and claimable commands → below-threshold alternative detected → overused profile command deferred with reason → other profile can obtain dispatch → affected agent tasks execute in revised order. - Boundary reachability: Source-owned RuntimeCommandDispatcher::dispatchcommand calls budgetgateoutcome; in Enforce mode calls deferclaimedcommandifowned, with a separate Shadow mode explicitly marked non-enforcing. This path is a shipped runtime fleet feature, not only a test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The threshold and fairness criterion are human-configured, and the dispatcher executes that criterion. The first-party source supplies a real cross-workflow interference-to-return path, but not a credited autonomous owner of S2 deliberation; hence constructor C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: At least two concurrently claimable distinct coding-agent runtime jobs/workflows, potentially assigned different provider/runtime profiles. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Saturating one active profile in shared dispatch slots while alternative active agent work is below its budget/usage band. - Attenuating coordination relation: Configured dailythrottlebreach compares profile spend and selectable pending alternative then conditionally yields/defers a dispatch slot to avoid starvation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Enforce-mode deferclaimedcommandifowned applies a barrier/backoff, allowing an under-threshold task to proceed first. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It uses measured inter-profile resource competition and reorders work based on other live claimable actors rather than merely following a hard-coded DAG or static dependency. - Evidence: dispatcherthrottle.rs:1-127, dispatcher.rs:375-500, dispatcher.rs:139-335. - Basis: Bounded constructible mutual coordination path with present enforcement wiring; owner config must supply policy. - Confidence: Medium for functional S2; distinct profile workload is supported but actual live contention not reproduced. - Caveats: If only one task/profile exists, nothing is mutually coordinated; Shadow does not enforce S2, and threshold policy does not originate autonomously.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No evidenced legitimate whole-current manager that makes binding tradeoffs across separate viable delivery units&#39; competing live commitments using a whole-system view. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Fleet cost/capacity pressure, PR/review backlog, stalled tasks and runtime-host outages, mostly handled by configured local barriers and per-workflow support. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator-configured budget ceilings and per-workflow cancel/unblock/retry are supported; no concrete cross-unit commitment/priority decision source uses a whole-current view and closes such a choice. - Decision owner: Operator for global threshold configuration and local intervention, deterministic workflow/dispatch machinery for applying them; not a first-party autonomous S3 governor or proven parent-owned S3-specific transaction at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Operator monitor, aggregate workflow health/status, registered runtime hosts, configured daily caps, queue budgets, status/repair control APIs. - Closure path: Workflow issue → per-workflow action; missing established fleet-level shared-commitment judgement based on whole-current status → allocated/renegotiated division resources → observed changed collective operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Observability and strong command execution alone do not demonstrate who decides system-wide resource bargains. The adaptive dispatch path addresses S2 contention under fixed limits, not S3 strategy for all commitments. - Whole-system current view: Runtime monitor aggregates active workflow states, action eligibilities, capacity and host health, but that is a support view, not itself a decision right. - Current-control decision scope: Configured workload budget and individual workflow cancellation/retry; insufficient evidence of actual whole-fleet priority/commitment reallocation tied to that view. - Evidence: mod.rs:167-275, mod.rs:486-575, dispatcher.rs:375-500, API contract. - Basis: Source-bounded absence of whole-current decision/closure under the examined modes. - Confidence: Medium; the operator UI is a credible future parent S3 surface but cannot be graded positive solely for existing. - Caveats: A human operator in an independently managed organization may use this cockpit for S3, but their unobserved off-platform governance cannot be credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Obtain independent current reality about model-agent-delivered coding work beyond the implementing agent&#39;s own completion summary and force corrective action before integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Erroneous claims of completed implementation, stale/dirty PR head, skipped requirements, faulty code, unresolved review threads, unvalidated changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separately invoked model-backed local reviewer inspects current repository head and original requirements, independently decides pass/changes/blocked and returns findings; first-party reducer moves the workflow accordingly. - Decision owner: Model-backed review agent in the separate local-review activity with independent review context; deterministic validation of signal/head/CI and GitHub snapshot is enforcement rather than the substantive model review. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Review activity, current-head SHA/clean-tree evidence, separate read-only review prompt, structured findings/signals, review-state reducer, queued repair task and exact-head readiness requirements. - Closure path: Implementer claims PR ready → independent local-review activity obtains current source/head and initial issue plan → LocalReviewChangesRequested or Blocked → workflow addressingfeedback with new remediation agent activity, or blocked → repaired current PR → new review/merge gate. - Boundary reachability: The shipped githubissuepr flow enqueues runlocalreview at a dedicated state, consumes LocalReviewOutcome and triggers addressprfeedback on findings, while a separate direct local reviewer runtime is supported. Not borrowed only from an external CI job. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model reviewer examines raw current PR independently and originates substantive findings; source-only signal parsing enforces the resulting audit decision and returns it. - Claim being audited: Agent-written completion/current PR readiness against original requirements and verified worktree at a specific commit. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementing coding agent&#39;s task/artifact/PR repair summary and claimed completion/validation. - Complementary access path: A separate fresh-context model-backed reviewer receives actual PR head/issue plan, checks working tree and evidence, and can refuse to accept the author&#39;s unsupported assertion. - Independence boundary: Separate activity/reviewer task from agent implementation and repair; independent review instructions explicitly require treating prior repairs as claims. The same provider family could be configured in two jobs, so independence is procedural, not guaranteed vendor separation. - Who acts on findings: The first-party workflow reducer queues further addressprfeedback, blocks or withholds readiness and can repeat local review before merge. - Evidence: prfeedback.rs:301-428, mod.rs:580-605, tick.rs:105-220. - Basis: Separate inquiry and authoritative blocking/correction return into current coding work, beyond routine implementer self-report. - Confidence: Medium-high source-structural; actual live PR review not run in this assessment. - Caveats: Ordinary tests/CI alone would not establish S3. Optional periodic review may corroborate but is not required for this independent local-review closed path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Observe repeated quality/environment failures, generate future-oriented new rule/skill or guard options, then allow valid learned policy to change subsequent harness operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing repository/project error patterns, repeated rule violations, chronic blockers, slow sessions and code hotspots exceed static prior protections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured GC coding agent examines gathered signals and chooses future remediation artifact content; bounded optional rules-only auto-adoption writes accepted artifacts, then scheduled first-party learning constructs rules into the current rules engine. - Decision owner: Model-backed GC adaptation agent for content selection; operator&#39;s standing rules-only path/prefix policy is a limit, not the individual remediation choice. First-party live learnrules model call can further select executable rule candidates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EventStore and scan checkpoint, signal detector and priority ranking, GC AgentRequest tool/budget limits, draft store, status/Pending/Adopted transitions, rule-only path gates, periodic selfevolution and rules-engine insertion. - Closure path: Prior recurring warnings/violations from real operation → sampled/aggregated signal → model-backed GC draft proposal → autoadoptmatching in opt-in RulesOnly mode and project-path validation → adopted draft → periodic learnrules agent produces parsed rules → rules engine addrule → new operational checks in later coding workflows. - Boundary reachability: QualityTrigger grades post-task observations and calls GcAgent.run; its optional autoadopt policy calls real filesystem adoption. The bundled server selfevolution::start schedules learnrules; handler reads adopted drafts and installs rule definitions into live engine. This is a shipped executable opt-in route, not a roadmap. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard-coded thresholds and parsers alone would not generate new context-sensitive remediations from emergent external errors. Source invokes a coding agent with observed signals, and separately a learning agent to turn adopted evidence into rules. - External distinction: Observed project execution quality, repository error patterns and recurring agent/tool failures outside the previous rule coverage. - Future / prospective distinction: Drafted guards/rules/skills are for later workflows, not merely retrying the same failed tool action. Adopted rule content changes subsequently enforced capability. - Adaptation option generated: New rule/guard/skill remediation derived from prior empirical warnings, with rules-only automatic adoption supported; defaults remain conservative and do not auto-adopt. - Path back into current capability / S3: Generated GC rule draft → bounded adoption → agent-extracted rule → state.engines.rules.write().await.addrule → later code-validation/rule-check operations (S1). There is no fabricated autonomous S3 just to close S4. - Evidence: gcagent.rs:106-222, gcagent.rs:260-363, qualitytrigger.rs:320-415, learn.rs:13-84, selfevolution.rs:25-103, misc.rs:180-235. - Basis: Model-selected prospective remedies reach a future runtime rule engine through shipped opt-in closure. - Confidence: Medium source-structural due to opt-in config and dependency on actual newly generated artifact validity. - Caveats: Default Off leaves pending drafts; no source claims the model always emits path-qualified eligible rule artifacts; runtime hosted inference and efficacy improvements not replicated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No evidenced ultimate identity/purpose constitutional arbitration with legitimate authority and actual return into global organizational policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Credential/network scope, project identity, task permissions, workflow configuration and merge authorization control execution safety, not the fundamental purpose/identity of the delivery organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Project operators configure credentials and standing rules and may approve a merge, but no in-boundary S5 actor resolves a purpose/identity conflict at the stated recursion. - Decision owner: External platform adopters set organizational mission; shipped runtime&#39;s rule engine, authentication and isolated tool permissions enforce delegated limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HTTP auth, agent capability contracts, workflow definitions, Starlark rules and execution sandbox. - Closure path: Operator config → policy enforcement; no detected identity crisis/proposal → ultimate legitimate authority → returned revised purpose guiding future cross-system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A gate that blocks access or asks for approval cannot turn into final constitutional authority merely because it is cryptographically or procedurally enforced. - Evidence: workflow.rs:72-95, misc.rs:180-235, API contract, dispatcher.rs:139-335. - Basis: First-party supported boundary absence, not an impossibility for a larger real organization. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Adopter corporate governance and contributor authorization are outside the first-party source boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c6e8a2f5d0677ca200001fe7f5bb01c85d029c61/assessments/rust-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c6e8a2f5d0677ca200001fe7f5bb01c85d029c61\">c6e8a2f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T15:35:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yuanchuan/aivo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aivo Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yuanchuan/aivo/tree/b26438571e80ae28fa489f07c2c22fd595e10269\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b26438571e80ae28fa489f07c2c22fd595e10269\">b264385 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work by interpreting a task, selecting coding/tools, executing them, observing results and iterating to a completed or typed terminal outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous codebases and requests, changing files/Git/process state, compiler/test/tool failures, permissions, provider responses, context pressure, interruptions, background work and implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what to inspect, which tool or subagent to invoke, what edit/command to perform next, how to react to returned evidence and when to finish or continue. - Decision owner: the main model-backed Aivo Code agent instantiated by AgentEngine. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party turn loop; file/process/search tools; plan/finish tools; MCP; skills; permissions; retries/guards; session persistence; context compaction; TUI/headless hosts. - Closure path: user task + current workspace/session → model selects action/tool → Aivo executes or gates it → result enters conversation → next model request sees the updated state → next action or terminal outcome. - Boundary reachability: aivo code and aivo code -e instantiate the same first-party engine directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed actor removes the open-ended coding judgment; deterministic runtime code remains transport/enforcement only. - Evidence: src/agent/engine/turn.rs; src/commands/engineassembly.rs; src/commands/codeagentoneshot.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference comes from an external provider; Aivo is credited for the first-party decision/tool/feedback organization around it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concrete interference among concurrently executing delegated coding S1s by separating their mutable repository state when the main agent judges isolation necessary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel file-mutating delegates can write the same shared workspace and collide, overwrite, or observe each other&#39;s partial changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide, in the model-authored subagent call, to request isolation: &quot;worktree&quot; for an editing delegate, especially when several delegates are fanned out in parallel. - Decision owner: the main model-backed Aivo Code agent. A profile may also predeclare isolation, but the standard tool contract independently exposes the same coordination decision to the agent at runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: concurrent subagent worker pool; model-visible isolation argument; disposable Git worktrees; per-delegate cwd; worktree guards/finalization; fail-closed refusal when requested isolation cannot be created for a parallel delegate. - Closure path: main agent identifies/fans out parallel delegated work → chooses worktree isolation for writers → Aivo creates separate worktrees before concurrent execution → delegates operate without shared-write collision → each result/worktree disposition returns to the parent → parent integrates or continues. - Boundary reachability: normal Aivo Code engine assembly advertises the subagent tool and its model-callable isolation: &quot;worktree&quot; argument; several leading subagent calls in one ordinary model tool batch enter the built-in parallel worker pool, so this is a shipped runtime path rather than test-only plumbing. - Distinct S1 units: two or more fresh delegated Aivo AgentEngine sub-engines running concurrently from one parent tool-call batch, each executing a bounded model/tool coding task. - Inter-S1 disturbance: parallel file-mutating delegates in the same checkout can collide through shared writes, overwrite or observe one another&#39;s partial changes, and make the combined workspace result unreliable. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent model selects worktree isolation for the affected delegates; Aivo binds each isolated child to a disposable Git worktree and, for a parallel delegate that requested isolation, refuses to fall back to the shared workspace if isolation cannot be created. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: isolation changes each child S1&#39;s actual workspace before it acts; after the parallel batch, each delegated result and worktree disposition returns as parent tool results so the main S1 can decide subsequent integration, review, or follow-up. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping rests on an explicit inter-S1 concurrent-write collision and a first-party worktree relation chosen to attenuate that disturbance. Parallel delegation, session mail, worker-pool scheduling and result fan-in are not credited by themselves. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime deterministically enforces the requested boundary, but the substantive choice that a delegate should be isolated is available to and selected by the main agent from the tool schema. Without that model decision, the generic path can run shared-workspace delegates. - Evidence: src/agent/engine/toolbatch.rs; src/agent/engine/subagent.rs; src/agent/builtinskills/create-agent.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Aivo does not force every parallel delegate into a worktree. The credited autonomous S2 mode is the supported runtime path where the agent explicitly chooses isolation to attenuate the documented writer-collision disturbance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-organization current-control function was established beyond the focal main coding agent&#39;s own task execution and bounded delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plans, budgets, permission state, context/step ceilings, subagent progress, background jobs and session messages are controlled operationally, but no separate whole-system current portfolio/resource/accountability control role is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: updateplan; finishturn; typed stops; subagent status; background jobs; permissions; TUI /goal; session mail; runtime guards. - Closure path: not applicable; no distinct whole-current state aggregation → metasystem intervention → changed multi-S1 operational allocation/accountability loop was reconstructed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main agent can delegate and observe bounded work while performing its own S1 task, but that does not by itself create a separate S3 function. - Evidence: src/agent/engine/turn.rs; src/agent/engine/toolbatch.rs; src/agent/engine/sessioncomms.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this negative state does not deny substantial lifecycle control inside a coding turn.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a producing coding agent&#39;s change/completion using a separate model-backed reviewer with complementary read-only access and evidence-backed verdicts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the producing agent may believe its implementation is correct or complete while the actual diff, tests, commands or edge cases reveal failure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the live workspace/change through a separate review context, decide PASS/FAIL or APPROVE/REQUEST CHANGES from observed evidence, and return that verdict/report to the parent agent. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed Aivo subagent instantiated from the compiled-in verification or evaluate profile. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in review profiles compiled into the binary; fresh delegated AgentEngine; no edit/write tools; read/grep/glob/list/run tools; real command/test execution; bounded subagent run; parent subagent result path; optional deterministic post-edit self-verification as corroborating evidence. - Closure path: producing main agent has a change/completion claim → invokes built-in verification/evaluate subagent → separate reviewer reads current workspace/diff and runs independent checks → reviewer emits an evidence-backed verdict → Aivo returns it as the parent tool result → main agent sees the verdict and can revise or reconfirm subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: the review profiles are compiled-in defaults discovered by normal Aivo Code engine assembly; no downstream custom reviewer implementation is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic Aivo code constructs the sub-engine and tool boundary, but the substantive audit findings/verdict are generated by a separate model-backed reviewer. Removing that reviewer removes the semantic audit judgment. - Evidence: src/agent/subagents.rs; src/agent/builtinagents/verification.md; src/agent/builtinagents/evaluate.md; src/agent/engine/subagent.rs; src/commands/engineassembly.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: review invocation is optional and model-selected; the state records a supported first-party audit mode, not a claim that every Aivo Code turn is independently reviewed. - Claim being audited: the producing main agent&#39;s implementation/change and its implied correctness/completion claim. - Ordinary reporting path: the producing agent&#39;s normal tool loop, text answer and finishturn completion path. - Complementary access path: a distinct sub-engine with no editing tools reads the live change/workspace and can run real tests/commands; edit-less review intentionally stays on the shared workspace so uncommitted producer changes remain visible. - Independence boundary: separate model invocation/session state, separate specialist system instructions and tool scope, no nested delegation, and no write/edit capability for the built-in verification/evaluate profiles. - Who acts on findings: the main Aivo Code agent receives the subagent&#39;s report as a tool result and controls subsequent coding/completion behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material autonomous outside-and-future intelligence loop that selects and persists an organizational adaptation was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: skills, agent profiles, session history, model/provider choice and project guides can change available capability/context, but the standard runtime does not autonomously scan future environmental change and adopt a persistent organizational redesign on that basis. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: user/project configuration or ordinary task-level model action, depending on the surface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent agent profiles; create-agent skill; skills; project guides; session state; MCP; model/provider configuration. - Closure path: not applicable; no environmental/future distinction → adaptation-option generation → autonomous selection → persistent capability/strategy change → later operation closure was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can author a profile when asked and Aivo can rediscover it mid-run, but generic authoring capability is not by itself a prospective S4 loop. - Evidence: src/agent/subagents.rs; src/agent/builtinskills/create-agent.md; src/commands/engineassembly.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream workflow may use Aivo tools to implement adaptation; that organization is outside this standalone assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, auto-approval, sandbox boundaries, project guides, model/provider settings, agent profiles and hooks constrain behavior, but they are operating policy/configuration rather than a reconstructed identity-level governance function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: user/operator/project configuration for the relevant policies; Aivo runtime enforces them and agents operate within them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission ladder; grants; sandbox escalation; hooks; project guides; model/provider configuration; agent profiles; approval UI/headless fail-closed behavior. - Closure path: not applicable at S5 level; no genuine identity/policy conflict → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative identity decision → returned operation loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither the main coding model nor review subagents own Aivo Code&#39;s ultimate identity/policy boundary. - Evidence: src/agent/permission.rs; src/commands/engineassembly.rs; src/commands/codeagentoneshot.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human/operator policy can govern a broader organization that uses Aivo; that parent is not imported without a function-specific parent-mode closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b5364bd04c506e6a4f267e549f8731cf016a0a50/assessments/aivo-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b5364bd04c506e6a4f267e549f8731cf016a0a50\">b5364bd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T23:44:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cft0808/edict\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Edict</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cft0808/edict/tree/14a207557719c046af0f993a7bff1cc5a5015b33\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 14a207557719c046af0f993a7bff1cc5a5015b33\">14a2075 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute a scoped ministry task (engineering, docs, analysis, testing or infrastructure) and return an observed outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool and code errors, variable repository state, incomplete outputs and other task-specific uncertainties. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A supported installed model-backed ministry agent chooses task actions/tools and adjusts behavior after observations and results. - Decision owner: The model-backed installed OpenClaw ministry agent; Edict supplies its bundled role and task-state interface, not the external model runtime&#39;s entire governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Installed role SOUL.md, OpenClaw agent workspace, subagent permission matrix, file-backed task/log commands and progress reporting. - Closure path: Assigned task → ministry agent tool work and observation → further action or completion → returned result and Edict task/progress state. - Boundary reachability: install.sh packages role prompts and configures the existing OpenClaw agents; ministry roles and delegation are an explicit supported full-install workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choices of implementation, testing and task-specific actions are model-directed in the composed installed mode; deterministic state files and dashboard do not themselves make choices. - Evidence: installer roles, work allocation and return contract, one ministry role. - Basis: Shipped provider-composed agent feedback/action mode. - Confidence: Medium-high for install path and role capability; no live task execution measured. - Caveats: Running the demo-only Docker dashboard does not yield this state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct closed first-party cross-ministry interference attenuation path is established beyond role assignment and fixed task routing at the reviewed installed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple ministry S1 workers could duplicate edits, compete for tools or produce incompatible changes, but a clear inter-S1 conflict detector and feedback resolution mechanism is not shown in the credited mode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not established as S2-specific in the first-party installed path. The model-backed Shangshu assigns what to each ministry at S3, and the gate reviews plans; ordinary messages and permissions do not show a conflict-driven coordinator relation. - Decision owner: No separate S2 owner evidenced. Experimental Redis dispatch semaphores are mechanical agent/task capacity and duplicate-delivery control, not autonomous inter-S1 conflict judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Department jurisdiction table, subagent allowAgents matrix, task/flow logs, status board and optional event-worker per-agent semaphore. - Closure path: Normal delegation and collection return to Shangshu, but no demonstrated cross-S1 interference signal → resolution → changed sibling operations is independently closed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A division of ministries, shared data and an execution queue are not sufficient under Methodology 0.3.7 without the specific inter-S1 disturbance witness. - Evidence: ministry routing, fixed OpenClaw permissions, separate backend dispatch limit. - Basis: Bounded negative: role routing is not independently evidenced S2 conflict attenuation. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: User-specific OpenClaw subagent extensions might implement stronger conflict management; those are not attributed to Edict.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Govern current commitments of one edict by selecting ministry assignments, monitoring their progress and deciding how to integrate or re-dispatch current work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Heterogeneous workload, partially finished ministry outputs, misassigned tasks, missing results and current delivery failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The installed model-backed Shangshu dispatch role decides which ministries undertake subtasks and how to respond to their results; the planning role can revise the current plan after gate rejection. - Decision owner: The composed OpenClaw Shangshu/Zhongshu control actors, distinct from the fixed transition script and operator dashboard. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan/todo state, assigned ministries, shared task files, progress reports, allowAgents, dashboard status and OpenClaw subagent call return. - Closure path: Whole-edict task/plan view → selected dispatch across specialized S1 cells → progress/results from ministries → Shangshu synthesizes and returns to Zhongshu/user or requests further work. - Boundary reachability: The first-party installer wires all referenced roles and their allowable delegations; bundled Shangshu instructions explicitly require progress inspection, assignment and aggregate response. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Selection of the department portfolio for a particular edict is agent discretion; predetermined state ordering and dispatch event handling are only carriers. - Whole-system current view: The edict&#39;s agreed plan, department task assignments, cross-ministry progress updates and result summaries under one edict ID; not every independent deployment or organization-wide roadmap. - Current-control decision scope: Departmental resource commitments, who acts now, synthesis and return of ongoing work, plus bounded recovery/replanning of the same edict. - Evidence: Shangshu current-control instructions, role composition, task-state map. - Basis: Shipped control-role discretion returned into current multi-ministry activity. - Confidence: Medium; no actual model-driven portfolio decision was tested. - Caveats: Scope is one edict, not project-level whole-portfolio S3. Manual stop/cancel/resume does not separately justify (P).\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Audit proposed operational plan claims independently of the planning actor before releasing the task to execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unworkable steps, missing subtasks, underestimated risks and inaccurate planning assumptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The separately configured Menxia review agent decides to approve (准奏) or veto (封驳) and return concrete corrective objections; rejection routes back to Zhongshu. - Decision owner: Model-backed Menxia agent in a separate installed OpenClaw role/workspace, not the Zhongshu planner whose claims it audits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Review rubric, command to return task state to Zhongshu or advance to Assigned, flow logs, allowAgents and finite retry guidance. - Closure path: Zhongshu plan → Menxia&#39;s separate feasibility/completeness/risk/resource inspection → veto/approval → re-planning or authorized execution. - Boundary reachability: Menxia workspace and role are installed by the standard script and permitted in the Zhongshu delegation chain; its SOUL.md gives executable board/return commands. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Menxia owns the plan-quality verdict, while transition scripts only record/enforce the chosen result. Reviewer independence is role/workspace and contrary authority, not proven unrelated model weights or institution. - Claim being audited: The planning department&#39;s proposed decomposition is sound and executable. - Ordinary reporting path: Zhongshu writes planning steps/todos and presents its proposed scheme. - Complementary access path: Separate Menxia role reviews the proposal under feasibility, completeness, risk and resource criteria and writes its own review result. - Independence boundary: Different installed agent identity, workspace, prompt and assigned role, with explicit veto even against the planner. It remains inside the same operator/provider ecosystem. - Who acts on findings: Zhongshu revises after a veto; approvals permit Shangshu to allocate work. - Evidence: Menxia audit criteria and executable veto, first-party role installation and permissions, process architecture. - Basis: A distinct review actor judges another controller&#39;s operational claim with direct return to the current control cycle. - Confidence: Medium; prompt-described veto is not a measured runtime guarantee. - Caveats: Menxia instructions force approval on the third review round; that seriously bounds the integrity of the gate and must not be described as an unconditional permanent veto. No live review efficacy study.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No evidenced first-party loop that turns sensed external/future environmental changes into adopted future delivery-system capability decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Emerging models, news, tooling and external requirements; the separately bundled news official can collect world/AI headlines, but news delivery is not yet an adaptation decision for Edict&#39;s task organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No S4-specific actor with option/adoption authority evidenced. User-triggered model changes and routine retrospective task choices remain separate paths. - Decision owner: Humans manually choose model assignments and skills; the brief agent selects news items, not Edict&#39;s future operating strategy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: News briefing official, headlines script, dashboard model selection, skill management and change application/restart script. - Closure path: News → digest for emperor does not show prospectively developed organizational options → authoritative adoption → changed future capability. Directly applying a user&#39;s selected model is configuration, not external intelligence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even a model-produced daily external digest need not constitute S4 unless its distinctions influence a future-oriented capability/strategy decision. - Evidence: news brief role, operator model-change script. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting plausible external-sensing and future-change surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Another external workflow could consume the daily brief and choose organizational adaptations, but none is shown as an operating first-party Edict mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy dispute handling and authoritative returned decision is established at the user-edict organization recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Disagreement over organizational mandate, legitimacy or immutable operating principles might arise, but installed role permissions handle delegation and task scope instead. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No actual ultimate-policy right exercised through a first-party agent or human parent escalation workflow is shown. The Emperor/user issues objectives and can intervene operationally, not evidenced identity-policy settlements. - Decision owner: Outside operator sets initial requirements, role allowlists and model preferences; no first-party S5-specific governing channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role permission matrix, separation-of-powers prompts, dashboard stop/cancel/resume, task final approval and local OpenClaw configuration. - Closure path: These mechanisms change one task or allowlist, but no identity-level conflict → legitimate authority → ultimate decision → standing subsequent policy loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Invoking imperial titles, vetting one plan or applying an operational safety rule does not establish governance of organizational identity. - Evidence: installer permissions, README intervention feature, task transition model. - Basis: Bounded absence of identity/ultimate-policy closure, including parent path. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An external human institution operating Edict may legitimately maintain S5, but the integration is not shown in these supported role/board paths.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f84d2a046b54a5ddd9fac72637281dcd530a5afe/assessments/ed/edict.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f84d2a046b54a5ddd9fac72637281dcd530a5afe\">f84d2a0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T13:34:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bigduu/Bamboo-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bamboo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bigduu/Bamboo-agent/tree/eb1151378d513e820f80513c387056fb14f5c664\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision eb1151378d513e820f80513c387056fb14f5c664\">eb11513 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed work through a persistent model/tool feedback loop and alter the local/external task environment through first-party tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user tasks, workspace/process state, tool results and failures, model/provider responses, context limits, permissions, memory/skill context and follow-up observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool/action and arguments, interpret returned evidence, and decide whether to continue, delegate or conclude. - Decision owner: the active root Bamboo model actor; a child model separately owns operational choices inside its delegated assignment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime loop, tool registry/executor, durable session state, permissions/approvals, context compression, memory, skill/MCP surfaces, execution/recovery machinery and child runtime. - Closure path: user/task/session context → model chooses substantive tool/action → runtime executes/refuses and records result → later model round observes the result → model revises action or returns a final result. - Boundary reachability: the ordinary Bamboo server/runtime ships this model/tool execution path directly; no downstream component is required to obtain the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime components execute, persist and constrain actions, but removing the model removes task-specific action selection and interpretation of operational feedback. - Evidence: README.md; crates/app/bamboo-server-tools/src/subagent.rs; ordinary runtime/engine architecture at the pinned ref. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference and redundant/overlapping work among concurrently executing child S1 units by assigning explicit non-overlapping responsibilities and mutation scopes, then steering or correcting children as live outcomes reveal conflicts or stalls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel children may default to the same parent workspace and can otherwise duplicate work, pursue overlapping responsibilities, modify overlapping state, or continue on a stale/wrong assignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide each child&#39;s responsibility, detailed task, workspace and relevant mutation boundary, and revise/steer/cancel that assignment when child status or outcomes show a coordination problem. - Decision owner: the root Bamboo model invoking the model-facing SubAgent tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: required responsibility field; schema guidance requiring narrow non-overlapping responsibility; child assignment frame making scope authoritative and restricting mutation to assigned scope; optional separate child workspace; background parallel execution; status guidance; sendmessage, update, cancel, wait and completion feedback. - Closure path: root model partitions work into explicit child responsibilities/workspaces → child assignment makes that scope authoritative during operation → concurrent children execute → current status/completion/error returns to root → root can redirect/retry/cancel/change scope → later child/root operation follows the revised coordination decision. - Boundary reachability: SubAgent is a standard first-party model-facing root tool and its child assignment/status machinery is wired through the shipped server/engine runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Bamboo supplies deterministic lifecycle and persistence, but the task-specific responsibility/workspace split and live redirection decisions are chosen by the root model. Removing the model leaves no equivalent semantic partition of the current job. - Evidence: crates/app/bamboo-server-tools/src/subagent.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/sessionapp/childsession/helpers.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/sessionapp/childcompletioncoordinator.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Bamboo does not establish a hard per-file disjoint-write lease at this ref; the credited attenuation is the model-owned non-overlapping responsibility/mutation-scope contract plus live steering, so badly chosen scopes can still collide. - Distinct S1 units: separately persisted root/child model-tool sessions; multiple children may be created in one turn and run concurrently in background. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent children may share the parent workspace and could duplicate or overlap work/state mutation without explicit task boundaries. - Attenuating coordination relation: required narrow, non-overlapping model-selected responsibilities plus authoritative per-child scope/mutation instructions and optional workspace separation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child status/results/errors return to the root; status guidance and sendmessage/update/cancel/rerun paths let the root alter the affected child&#39;s subsequent execution rather than blindly spawning redundant work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is not child spawning or mailbox availability alone; it is explicitly aimed at separating concurrent responsibilities/mutations that can interfere in a shared workspace and closing observed problems through changed child behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current root/child organization as a whole, allocate active child commitments/resources and intervene in live child work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing child portfolio, running/pending/error/completed state, stalls, wrong assignments, model/cost tradeoffs, need for early error response and changing current task commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create or withhold child commitments; select responsibility, workspace, model, reasoning effort and lifecycle; inspect current children; update/rerun/steer/cancel/delete them; and choose whole-set wait policy before integrating results. - Decision owner: the root Bamboo model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubAgent.list/get/create/update/run/sendmessage/cancel/delete/wait; child index/session persistence; runtime status guidance; wait policy; completion coordinator and automatic parent resume. - Closure path: root creates current child portfolio → children execute and expose current state/results → root lists/gets or waits on the portfolio → root changes assignments/resources/steering or terminates work as needed → runtime applies the chosen intervention and subsequent operation proceeds under the new commitments. - Boundary reachability: these controls are available directly to the root model through the ordinary shipped SubAgent tool; they do not depend on test/dev orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: wait scheduling, persistence and cancellation mechanics enforce decisions, but the root model owns the discretionary allocation and intervention choices over the current child organization. - Evidence: crates/app/bamboo-server-tools/src/subagent.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/sessionapp/childsession/helpers.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/sessionapp/childcompletioncoordinator.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: a simple single-agent run does not exercise S3; the state is established in the shipped multi-child mode. No distinct parent-governed S3 mode was established from the inspected operator/UI surfaces. - Whole-system current view: list exposes the active child roster and get/completion/status machinery exposes each child&#39;s current or terminal state; wait can reason over every active child or an explicit subset under a root-selected policy. - Current-control decision scope: current child commitments, responsibilities, workspaces, model/reasoning allocation, lifecycle, steering, retries, cancellation/deletion and wait/integration timing.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently challenge the root agent&#39;s terminal completion claim using a separate read-only adversarial reviewer that inspects workspace evidence and returns an approve/reject verdict with findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the executing agent can claim completion despite missed requirements, incorrect changed files, inadequate diff/test evidence, regressions or unverified criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect changed files and recorded verification evidence, decide whether stopping is justified, and reject with concrete findings that must be addressed before completion. - Decision owner: a separate Guardian reviewer model in the implemented constructor path; the ordinary server exposes no activation owner because it passes guardianconfig: None at this ref. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only Guardian child tool denylist, dedicated adversarial rubric, durable Guardian state, bounded review budget, verdict parser, synthetic reject on malformed/error output, completion coordinator and hidden parent-resume message. - Closure path: when the constructor is enabled, terminal point → runtime spawns read-only Guardian → Guardian independently reads/searches changed-file and recorded diff/test evidence → verdict is recorded → rejection resumes parent with findings and directs repair → a later terminal point can be reviewed again. The standard server stops before the trigger because Guardian configuration is not surfaced. - Boundary reachability: the complete S3-specific machinery and server-provided spawner are first-party and shipped, but crates/app/bamboo-server/src/handlers/agent/execute/runtime/execution.rs explicitly supplies guardianconfig: None with a TODO to expose configuration. A downstream/first-party composition step is therefore still required to activate the audit loop, so the publication state is Constructor rather than Autonomous. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent reviewer would own the audit judgment once activated; deterministic parsing/resume only enforces its verdict. No standard server run can currently instantiate that ownership mode from the supported request surface. - Evidence: crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/runtime/guardianstate.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/sessionapp/childcompletioncoordinator.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/runtime/config.rs; crates/app/bamboo-server/src/handlers/agent/execute/runtime/execution.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: this is intentionally C, not A: engine completeness is not enough to borrow an activation path absent from the assessed server mode. - Claim being audited: the main/root agent&#39;s claim that the current task is complete and the run may terminate. - Ordinary reporting path: main agent reaches terminal completion using its own execution context and recorded work/test evidence. - Complementary access path: a separate read-only Guardian child with dedicated Read/Glob/Grep/GetFileInfo-style inspection access evaluates the actual changed files plus recorded diff/test evidence rather than trusting the main claim. - Independence boundary: distinct child session/model context, read-only tool surface, explicit adversarial rubric and separate verdict state; mutation/spawn/shell/web tools are removed. - Who acts on findings: runtime records the verdict and resumes the root model with concrete findings; the root then repairs/reverifies before another terminal attempt.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: detect recurring operational demand across distinct successful sessions and construct a candidate reusable Skill for future work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated successful multi-step workflows remain ad hoc and repeatedly consume reasoning/tool orchestration instead of becoming a reusable future capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: recognize that a normalized successful tool sequence has repeated across enough distinct sessions and emit a proposed reusable Skill representation with validation evidence. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party ReuseDraftCollector owns detection/candidate generation; no autonomous or parent actor is wired to admit the candidate into the live skill catalog at this ref. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: successful-root-trace extraction, canonical tool/value-shape normalization, stable signature, distinct-session threshold, review artifact generation, persisted SKILL.md candidate and validation examples; separate SkillStore discovery/live reload for actual admitted skill roots. - Closure path: successful root sessions → repeated normalized trace crosses threshold → Bamboo emits review-only reuse-drafts/&lt;candidate&gt;/SKILL.md and validation artifact → constructor boundary stops here. The draft is intentionally outside every discovery root, so an additional promotion decision/path is required before later runs can use it as a live Skill. - Boundary reachability: repeated-trace observation is wired directly into first-party SkillManager and produces the candidate without downstream code. Because admission into current capability is deliberately omitted, the shipped path establishes S4 construction but not autonomous or parent-governed closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: candidate detection/generation is deterministic rather than an autonomous S4 actor, and neither the root model nor a first-party parent approval surface is shown owning promotion into live capability. This is therefore C, not A/P. - Evidence: crates/infra/bamboo-skills/src/reusedraft.rs; crates/infra/bamboo-skills/src/lib.rs; crates/infra/bamboo-skills/src/store/mod.rs; builtinskills/skill-creator/SKILL.md; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/gardener.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: user-directed skill-creator can produce and iterate Skill artifacts, and SkillStore live-reloads actual discovery roots, but no first-party reviewed path was found that promotes automatic reuse drafts into those roots. Memory gardeners maintain memory quality rather than providing this missing adaptation authority. - External distinction: repeated successful root sessions encode recurring user/environment demand across distinct operating episodes rather than one current task; the reuse collector deliberately aggregates across distinct sessions. - Future / prospective distinction: the generated artifact is explicitly a proposed reusable Skill intended to change how future matching work can be performed. - Adaptation option generated: a proposed Skill definition/SKILL.md plus validation artifact derived from the recurring tool sequence. - Path back into current capability / S3: not closed at this ref; drafts are intentionally written beside, not inside, the configured Skill discovery root. Promotion into a live-reloaded Skill root requires an additional composition/authority path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the declared Bamboo deployment boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Bamboo exposes many task goals, system instructions, permission policies, tool/skill disablement settings and human approvals, but these regulate current actions/capabilities rather than resolving the identity or ultimate-policy questions of the organization itself. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy right and closed return path were established. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompts, permission checker/approvals, tool/skill configuration, Gold goal configuration, updategoal, runtime budgets and Guardian/skill constructor policies. - Closure path: no qualifying S5 issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative identity/policy decision → returned operational-governance loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root model can make task decisions and report goal completion, but it does not own the system&#39;s identity or ultimate policy. Operator configuration/approval likewise constrains actions without evidencing S5 closure. - Evidence: crates/engine/bamboo-tools/src/tools/goal.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/runtime/config.rs; README.md; inspected server permission/configuration and skill/memory surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/311ccc4918a19b810674ae2685de8f0bded93a88/assessments/bamboo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 311ccc4918a19b810674ae2685de8f0bded93a88\">311ccc4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T19:26:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andrew-yangy/gru-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">gruAI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andrew-yangy/gru-ai/tree/8fba47928a0fe3fdfda7a31d22b73625c78cbfc4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8fba47928a0fe3fdfda7a31d22b73625c78cbfc4\">8fba479 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-driven specialist engineering, auditing, designing and other directive tasks within their scoped project contexts, with tool observations and revision cycles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing requirements, source defects, failing tests, review findings and variable task complexity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next code/document/tool action on the scoped goal and revise it after tool/reviewer observations. - Decision owner: spawned model-based Claude Code role agents (engineer/QA/CTO etc.) operating under first-party .claude/agents personas and /directive work instructions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code CLI tool executor, role templates, task Definition of Done, isolated worktrees, step status JSON, reviews and safety hooks. - Closure path: assigned goal/DOD → spawned model worker selects actions and reads results → evaluator returns issues → worker fixes/rechecks → completed artifact or escalation to orchestrator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-specific decisions reside in model roles under the shipped task contract; the CLI adapter only launches them and does not autonomously solve tasks. - Evidence: README pipeline, /directive, 09-execute-projects.md, scripts/spawn-agent.ts, role personas and Claude CLI adapter. - Basis: explicit source-to-spawn-to-review control path, not an inferred model feature from names. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: live provider quality and all claimed automated end-to-end runs unverified; role sessions require installed Claude Code and access. - Boundary reachability: the init scaffold installs the skill/roles and /directive invokes them through the supported CLI adapter; results feed project state and review phases.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent concrete cross-worker file/edit collisions while allowing parallel non-overlapping S1 work, and enforce separation of builder/reviewer roles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two builders changing overlapping files, global configuration file edits and incompatible simultaneous dependency work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: sequence conflicting S1 tasks into different waves and allow only disjoint file scopes to execute in one parallel wave. - Decision owner: constructor-specified computeWaves algorithm in the shipped execution protocol, executed by the orchestrating agent; static global sequential file set and file-overlap rule bound decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: audit activefiles[], task dependencies, MAXPARALLEL=3, overlap detection, post-wave changed-file comparison and reviewer identity validation. - Closure path: pending S1 task scopes → compute dependency/file-conflict wave → launch safe workers → compare changed files, review/repair → subsequent wave eligible after prior tasks finish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the attenuation rule is constructor-authored, not a free model-authorized cross-team strategy; nonetheless it addresses specific interference between autonomous S1 writers. - Distinct S1 units: per-task Claude Code builder sessions, including concurrently active specialist agents on the same project tree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping activefiles or shared global config operations can corrupt/overwrite another worker&#39;s changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: deterministic wave planner refuses concurrent file intersections and serializes global modification files, bounded by dependency readiness. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: waves complete and changed files are compared to the predicted set; remaining tasks enter later waves only after prior completions, and reviews feed repairs back to builders. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the explicit file-overlap interference model changes which otherwise-ready independent S1 tasks can act simultaneously, preventing destructive concurrent edits rather than merely passing messages. - Evidence: 09-execute-projects.md wave algorithm, 05-planning.md cast validation, .claude/hooks/validate-cast.sh and validate-reviews.sh. - Basis: explicit normative executable instructions plus gate scripts in the installed workflow. - Confidence: medium; protocol specifies the algorithm but there is no independent measured production collision benchmark at this pinned source. - Caveats: post-wave drift is warning-only; S2=C is bounded to specified scheduling and role constraints, not proof of comprehensive runtime concurrency safety. - Boundary reachability: /directive execute phase requires the wave manifest and uses scripts/spawn-agent.ts for parallel CLI builders; conflicts influence later planned waves.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model-led COO orchestration of the present directive portfolio: challenge scope, audit state, assign role agents, select plan, reprioritize, review outcomes and escalate when gates fail. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing goals, active file scope, task dependencies, unavailable workers, repeated review failures, cost and inconsistent project progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and revise the current execution plan/cast across multiple specialist S1 workers, determine when to stop/rescope and return a corrected directive state. - Decision owner: the first-party COO model persona and directive orchestrator under the user&#39;s approved intent, with CTO/QA evidence inputs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: audit artifacts, plan.json, project.json, directive.json, step dependency guards, reviewer artifacts, task statuses, dashboard watcher and scripted scheduler capacity cap. - Closure path: CEO directive/current state → audit/COO whole-plan judgment → assign and sequence specialist tasks → collect review and completion feedback → adjust/escalate or present digest to CEO. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the COO decides the active plan, casting, phasing and tradeoffs from source/audit evidence; separate scripts only validate/record that current control. - Whole-system current view: directive goals, projects, DOD statuses, candidate agents, current backlog, lessons and budget/role constraints assembled before COO plan and subsequent reports. - Current-control decision scope: model-based allocation of builders/reviewers, sequencing, risk/escalation and present directive work; external CEO alone owns final acceptance and high-risk authorization. - Evidence: morgan-coo.md, /directive/SKILL.md, 05-planning.md, 09-execute-projects.md, 10-wrapup.md and README. - Basis: encoded role/planning and return architecture with checkable state contracts. - Confidence: medium-high for available mode; no production performance assertion. - Caveats: this is bounded current work control, not business-wide governance of every linked product or independent authority to approve a release. - Boundary reachability: /directive explicitly invokes COO planning, writes approved project plan, launches roles, reads returned reviews and progresses to a digest/CEO gate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: mandatory task peer review, QA and mechanical casting/review validations improve quality but do not evidence an independent complementary audit institution of the whole S3 control loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-review, incomplete DOD, hidden implementation regressions and omitted verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently test the current S3 controller&#39;s account of actual organization-wide operations and return a binding management challenge. - Decision owner: assigned CTO/reviewer inside normal task workflow; no separate first-party independent S3 actor with complementary access to S3 is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh-context code-review prompts, validate-reviews.sh, validate-cast.sh, project DOD and review artifacts. - Closure path: builder artifact → reviewer observations → builder repair/ordinary gate → COO completion flow; not a distinct system-wide S3 → S3 challenge path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent task review is not automatically independent organizational audit; reviewers are selected by the current planning chain. - Evidence: 09-execute-projects.md, validate-reviews.sh, 05-planning.md. - Basis: separate task-check vs complementary whole-system audit test. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: product claims about fresh contexts do not independently attest reviewer independence in a deployed environment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: model-led prospective scanning of external technology, competition, user and ecosystem signals, with synthesis into prioritized future directives and possible operational adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes to frameworks/security advisories, competitor moves, emerging markets, user needs and prospective workflow technologies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autonomously select external research questions, weigh competing signals and propose initiatives/capability changes for authorized backlog/directive promotion. - Decision owner: first-party CTO/CPO/CMO/COO model scouts and COO consolidator; human CEO retains acceptance of ordinary proposals and high-risk decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WebSearch/WebFetch instructions, prior scouting archive, goal alignment, urgency schema, cross-scout signal strengths, backlog triggers, /scout and scheduled claude -p entry. - Closure path: external developments → multi-domain agent research → independent proposed actions → COO cross-domain relevance/urgency synthesis → CEO review or bounded strong-signal inbox promotion → approved directive executed by S1/S3 pipeline. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research queries, interpretation, consolidation and prioritized option selection are explicit model-agent responsibilities; script scheduling and CEO gate are support and authorization, respectively. - Prospective environment view: technology CVEs and releases, competition/customer signals, growth landscape and workflow ecosystem not just current repo health. - Future option/adaptation decision: propose ranked specific initiatives with scope, evidence, risk and process; cross-reference pending backlog triggers to promote actionable work. - Return into operating capability: proposals accepted by CEO, plus specified strong signals, create/promo inbox directives that the first-party /directive workflow can act on; adoption is not guaranteed without authorization. - External distinction: scouts search competitors, platform advisories, market/customer signals and future framework choices, rather than merely the local build&#39;s current defects. - Future / prospective distinction: they assess changes that might affect later product viability and workflow capabilities; the goal is option selection before commitment, not incident response to an already assigned task. - Adaptation option generated: source schema requires evidence-linked prioritized initiatives with risk, recommended process and scope; COO can promote validated multi-scout signals to inbox directives. - Path back into current capability / S3: approved or bounded automatically promoted initiative becomes a directive that the active COO-and-specialists operating workflow can plan and execute, subject to CEO gates. - Evidence: /scout/SKILL.md, scripts/scheduled-runs.sh, COO persona, /directive consumption path and README. - Basis: implemented, reachable prospective scout protocol with bounded return, not mere roadmap language. - Confidence: medium; live search/approval/execution was not performed. - Caveats: hardcoded example installation paths require deployment configuration; not proof autonomous approval of strategic changes, and model-generated prospective findings may be wrong. - Boundary reachability: /scout is shipped as a first-party skill, scripts/scheduled-runs.sh scout invokes it via Claude CLI, and its initiatives/trigger promotions feed the same .context directive/backlog structure used by normal execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: the CEO&#39;s vision, standing preferences, approval gates and agent personas constrain execution but do not create first-party model authority to resolve ultimate identity/policy conflicts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous purpose, unsafe high-risk changes, conflicting priorities and mistakes in approval or scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimately adjudicate contested mission/identity and constitutional policy, then bind the operating organization to the decision. - Decision owner: external human CEO defines vision and must approve plan/completion; model personas obey inherited constraints and cannot unilaterally change them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: .context/vision.md, preferences, triage/classification rules, human clarification/plan/completion stop gates and validate-gate.sh. - Closure path: external CEO decision → scaffolded policy and approval status → bounded S1/S3 execution; no independent first-party ultimate-policy judgment and constitution-changing return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming the user CEO and the model COO does not demonstrate that software legitimately owns organizational S5. The explicit mandatory human gate identifies the outside owner. - Evidence: /directive/SKILL.md, 11-completion-gate.md, 07-plan-approval.md, README. - Basis: decision-right and human-stop trace. - Confidence: high for bounded exclusion. - Caveats: real-world team could include its human CEO in a broader assessed organization; no automatic parent-assisted autonomy credit is inferred here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3da53146a6df1cee901878d25327deb8991d105e/assessments/gr/gruai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3da53146a6df1cee901878d25327deb8991d105e\">3da5314 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T17:19:38+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensesh/KARIMO\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KARIMO</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensesh/KARIMO/tree/305abbe8d7a86d01023913cd12072cd83e937f52\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 305abbe8d7a86d01023913cd12072cd83e937f52\">305abbe &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Research codebase/external technical context, prepare task briefs and implement/test/document concrete feature changes in an actual project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Source/API uncertainty, contradictory requirements, failing tests, changing files, dependencies and task-specific errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Installed model-backed researcher, interviewer, implementer, tester and documenter roles choose project inspections, edits, tests and corrections within assigned briefs; outputs return as research artifacts, commits, test evidence and PR results. - Decision owner: Selected first-party KARIMO agent role operating on the Claude Code Task/tool host, not Bash alone or the generic Claude runtime acting without the role configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent frontmatter and tools, file-boundary rules, worker worktrees, task brief, gh, git, tests, status and CI. - Closure path: User feature request → research/PRD/brief → PM&#39;s task assignment → model worker reads actual code and chooses write/check actions → source/test/commit/PR result → PM and ultimately user. - Boundary reachability: The marketplace declares the plugin and /karimo:run explicitly spawns PM and worker types. Implementer has Read/Write/Edit/Bash tools, source-inspection instructions, validation and commit/PR handoff. External generic Claude Code cognition is not claimed as an independent KARIMO operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model-backed workers leaves install/manifest/branch scripts and static instructions but no task-specific product research, code design, chosen edits or semantic repair. - Evidence: manifest and plugin declaration; run&#39;s installed spawn path; worker task/tool/validation contract; worker implementation and test return; research role and technical source access. - Basis: First-party installed model/tool agent configurations linked through the normal task workflow. - Confidence: High for specified operational ownership path; no generated-code-quality measurement. - Caveats: Model, host and GitHub permissions must exist, and prompt-obedience is not verified by native shell tests. A feature may be run with fewer roles.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Pre-empt a concrete conflict between distinct planned workers whose briefs encode incompatible wave dependencies, overlapping files or mutually inconsistent acceptance criteria, so their subsequent execution instructions no longer collide. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two tasks placed in a parallel wave both modifying dependent/shared files, one worker expecting a file a later-wave worker must first create, or conflicting per-wave success criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party brief-reviewer inspects the cross-task briefs against actual source/dependency state and proposes correction; brief-corrector can modify task dependencies, briefs and wave preconditions. In the default run command the human must select the apply-fixes path before those corrections take effect, so autonomous end-to-end S2 attenuation is not established. - Decision owner: A composed brief-reviewer → user-choice → brief-corrector construction; there is no claimed autonomous live inter-worker conflict arbitrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dependency DAG, generated execution plan, file scopes, source/PRD read access, review findings, brief-corrector, model-backed worker prompts, serial wave gates, native worktree separation. - Closure path: Proposed parallel worker briefs and current code → reviewer spots a specific cross-task collision and produces actionable finding → user selects apply fixes → corrector alters tasks.yaml dependencies or briefs → PM dispatches changed instructions in subsequent execution waves. - Boundary reachability: The standard /karimo:run brief-review phase challenges task order, dependencies and file-boundary collisions, then exposes an explicit correction option wired to a shipped corrector. The positive claim is limited to this selected preparatory mode; --skip-review bypasses it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party source-aware conflict detector and editing agent can settle a specified cross-S1 task interference, but the essential default user choice means a fully autonomous S2 function is not shown. Generic wave ordering, isolated git worktrees, branch guards and PM scheduling alone would not satisfy S2. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more different planned implementer/tester/documenter workers whose briefs share or conflict over concrete source files and prerequisite outputs. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Same-wave edits or inconsistent order/acceptance contracts causing incompatible worker outputs, not generic task uncertainty or review quality. - Attenuating coordination relation: Source-aware cross-brief mismatch finding and explicit rewrite of dependency order / work brief before the named workers are dispatched. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Changed tasks.yaml dependency order and corrected briefs are loaded by PM, then affect worker wave placement and source modifications. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The witness is conflict detection against two named task contracts followed by a concrete correction of their interference, not the existence of a DAG, queue or mailbox. - Evidence: brief-review collision and original-code probes; shared-file sequencing checks; run&#39;s review and correction selection; corrector modifies dependencies and task briefs; deprecation of separate legacy conflict resolver. - Basis: Narrow source-constructed S2-specific pre-dispatch correction, with a necessary user choice; not measured runtime inter-worker reconciliation. - Confidence: Medium-low; preparatory planned-unit conflict correction is a narrower witness than live operational oscillation attenuation. - Caveats: No (P) state is used for S2. A user bypassing review or declining the correction lacks this path. A runtime git merge conflict is not itself credited as resolved by KARIMO; the deprecated review-architect is outside the default feature-mode claim.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Maintain the current PRD&#39;s full task/wave commitments, assign specialized workers, redirect failing tasks and decide current repair/gate/stop actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dependency stalls, worker failure and incomplete PRs, unexpected code/test/review results, shifting per-task complexity, worktree crashes and exhausted revision budget. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model-backed PM examines current PRD/task status and GitHub/git reality, chooses current worker allocation, wave progress, model escalation, corrective delegation and whether to pause/replan. It spawns pm-reviewer and pm-finalizer for specialized current subflows. Configured deterministic guards can prevent unsafe branch/wave transitions without themselves owning the decisions. - Decision owner: KARIMO karimo-pm role for the one running PRD; user retains product/merge authorization, and native Claude Code/CI only supply execution/check facilities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tasks.yaml, executionplan.yaml, status.json, findings.md, worktree/branch guards, provider result adapters, wave merge verification, human gate model and git-state crash reconstruction. - Closure path: PRD scope and task graph plus current worker/PR/test/review feedback → PM selects remaining worker/wave/rework/escalation commitment → task/tool dispatch or pause → changed PRD work and task/PR state return for subsequent decisions. - Boundary reachability: /karimo:run invokes this model-backed role in the shipped feature workflow. PM receives a whole-PRD status view and explicit authority over its task delegation, waves, review/repair and current stop/resume choices. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Branch assertions, automatic test gates and concurrency caps are fixed mechanisms; removing the PM model actor removes task-specific current prioritization, rerouting and correction decisions. - Whole-system current view: Current PRD/task graph, briefs, execution plan, model assignment, status.json, GitHub PR merge/review states, findings and open gates across the PRD&#39;s workers. - Current-control decision scope: Which task to execute/retry and with which worker/model, how to respond to review and stalls, what wave/gate can proceed, and whether this single PRD is ready or blocked; not global multi-project enterprise policy. - Evidence: PM owner and current scope; PRD-wide inputs and overlap check; current plan/collision probes; wave and worker delegation loop; PR/review return; terminal wave gate. - Basis: First-party configured model coordinator with current multi-worker commitment and feedback paths. - Confidence: Medium; source-wiring evidence, not observed live model control effectiveness. - Caveats: Initial user confirmation and optional human wave gates constrain execution. The role&#39;s embedded Bash snippets are instructions, not a verified standalone PM scheduler runtime. automergeongreen claims are deployment-dependent and do not establish an independent ultimate authority.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Challenge the preceding research/planning/brief agents&#39; readiness claims against the actual codebase and task requirements before executable commitments are handed to workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A brief writer&#39;s incorrect file assumptions, infeasible success criteria, missing prerequisite, contradictory tasks and project configuration mismatch not visible in a summary-only status signal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separately configured brief-reviewer uses Read/Grep/Glob/Bash to directly inspect first-party project files and distinguishes critical blockers from warnings, returning HOLD/clearance findings. The run command makes those findings part of the human-gated correction path and can dispatch a different brief-corrector before current work begins. - Decision owner: The model-backed KARIMO brief-reviewer for substantive pre-execution audit; it does not itself implement changes. The user/runner determines whether fixes are applied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate named agent and review step, direct repository artifact access, original PRD/brief inputs, structured findings, correction instructions and optional model-backed corrector. - Closure path: Brief writer&#39;s planned-task claims → independently invoked reviewer checks originals against codebase and cross-task ground truth → documented critical findings and execution clearance → run controller/user routes correction → amended briefs/PRD and ensuing PM task dispatch. - Boundary reachability: The normal /karimo:run flow explicitly spawns a distinct source-reading review role, saves recommendations and exposes corrections before the PM&#39;s execution stage. This is first-party audit of planned work, not first-party independent code audit of every produced PR. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model reviewer selects discrepancies and operational clearance from source evidence independently of brief author reports. A deterministic YAML validator or GitHub PR approval alone would not. Role/context separation is structural, not a proof of separate vendor, host isolation or actual quality. - Claim being audited: Research/brief-generation claim that task contracts reflect current files, dependencies and viable acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Researcher and brief writer submit consolidated findings, proposed task briefs and dependencies to /karimo:run/PM. - Complementary access path: Reviewer directly reads source code, config files and actual task briefs through its own Read/Grep/Glob/Bash tools, with explicit no-edit/no-commit rule. - Independence boundary: Different model-backed reviewer role/context with read-only instructions and its own clearance verdict; both use Claude Code host and may use same model family. - Who acts on findings: Run controller presents recommendations; user may select first-party corrector to alter tasks before PM delegates them. - Evidence: reviewer task/source-access rights; findings and clearance contract; run&#39;s separate audit and correction-return path; corrector&#39;s no-independent-investigation rule. - Basis: Default-connected, independent source-reading audit path for pre-execution planning claims, not optional external PR review. - Confidence: Medium, limited to the preparatory planning phase. - Caveats: The --skip-review option bypasses this audit; no guaranteed independent model/provider or sandbox separation is evidenced. External Greptile/Claude Code Review results routed by pm-reviewer are not credited as KARIMO&#39;s own independent source-review decision.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would monitor external/future conditions, select changes to the feature organization&#39;s enduring capability and return those adaptations into subsequent current operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future shifts in product needs, platform options or project environment, beyond technical evidence needed for today&#39;s PRD. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supported S4-specific future capability decision owner at this one-PRD recursion. Research examines external libraries for a named current feature; feedback investigates past/internal KARIMO failures after user prompting and applies user-approved learnings to future tasks. - Decision owner: No source-established autonomous future-policy owner for this PRD organization; human product architect chooses future features and rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: researcher WebSearch/WebFetch, /karimo:research external technical references, investigator drift checks, /karimo:feedback interviews, feedback auditor, persistent .karimo/learnings and per-PRD config. - Closure path: Feature research returns into present planning, and retrospective feedback may change future rules after user approval; neither is a differentiated external-prospective intelligence → autonomous organizational capability change → operational return at the declared recursion. - Boundary reachability: These are shipped options but their first-party contracts distinguish feature-focused knowledge gathering and after-the-fact human-triggered improvement, not a standing outside-and-then regulator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Web search or retention of a learned rule does not by itself establish anticipatory environment modelling or authority to change the organization&#39;s future capability. - Evidence: feature-scoped research requirement; external best-practice evidence used in PRDs; feedback is user-triggered and approval-gated; retrospective auditor and approval return. - Basis: Negative at the declared one-PRD recursion; nontrivial adjacent retrospective learning acknowledged. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: At a differently scoped long-lived parent organization, the feedback/learned-rule path could warrant dedicated S4 reassessment. No upgrade of the present PRD&#39;s research activity is inferred.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would adjudicate an identity/ultimate-purpose conflict and return a binding policy decision for the continuing organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts over the fundamental product purpose, standing authority, legitimacy or ultimate trade-offs beyond today&#39;s requested PRD. - Decisive decision or feedback right: KARIMO exposes requirement interviews, optional human approval gates, PR merge restrictions, branch/file safety rules and configuration choices, but no first-party identity-policy decision path. - Decision owner: Human project owner outside the assessed one-PRD operating organization retains these strategic/authorization rights; PM owns current task allocation, not enduring purpose. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLAUDE/KARIMO rules, PRD approval, gated execution, nevertouch/review boundaries, GitHub merge approval and review-provider configuration. - Closure path: User may authorize, pause, edit or merge the current feature; no evidence of an ultimate identity conflict deliberated and returned as organizational policy to subsequent PRDs. - Boundary reachability: Existing commands implement task acceptance and human limits, not identity-level governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Prompt rules, GitHub branch protection and ordinary operator assent are constraints, not a policy-setting agent or fully specified parent-governed S5 mechanism. - Evidence: run human approval and options; PM current scope; manual/auto merge command permissions. - Basis: Bounded source-negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A parent institution may exercise S5 for the project/company, but that would be another system in focus. No standalone P inferred from ordinary PR approvals.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d38427fb9fe4519340de4ee902fe1b0cd97ad478/assessments/ka/karimo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d38427fb9fe4519340de4ee902fe1b0cd97ad478\">d38427f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T17:08:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zeroclaw-labs/zeroclaw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ZeroClaw</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zeroclaw-labs/zeroclaw/tree/757db6c356c61861256a87222e30c7c92e8f166f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 757db6c356c61861256a87222e30c7c92e8f166f\">757db6c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user- or event-directed work through a model-driven tool/observation loop and return operational outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous requests, external/tool results, provider responses, failures, installed capabilities and changing task context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive next actions/tools and revise the plan from returned observations within configured authority. - Decision owner: the running ZeroClaw model agent; delegated configured agents own their delegated work at their recursion level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent loop, tool registry/dispatcher, security policy, approval bridge, provider transport, memory/context handling and tool receipts. - Closure path: inbound work → model decision → authorized tool/delegation action → observation/result → subsequent model decision → outcome. - Boundary reachability: the standard zeroclaw agent -a &lt;alias&gt;/channel runtime enters the first-party agent loop shipped in the ZeroClaw binary; no downstream harness is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code transports and constrains actions, while the model chooses substantive operational actions from current evidence. - Evidence: README.md, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/loop.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model inference is execution substrate, not an imported organizational owner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path was established that attenuates a concrete cross-S1 interference condition among distinct operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S2 at the reviewed boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegation policy, shared action/cost ceilings, depth limits, routing, task sequencing and parallel execution exist, but none is credited without a demonstrated S2-specific disturbance relation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the repository supports communication and work distribution among agents, but delegation/parallelism alone does not prove coordination in the VSM sense. - Evidence: docs/book/src/agents/delegation.md, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/delegate.rs. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: reassess if first-party evidence explicitly connects a coordination mechanism to shared-resource contention, oscillation or another cross-S1 disturbance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current portfolio of background work delegated by a configured caller agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing workload, long-running delegated tasks, completed/failed work and tasks that are no longer needed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create background delegated commitments, inspect/list caller-owned tasks, wait for selected tasks and cancel current work. - Decision owner: the model-driven caller agent using the built-in delegate control surface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: control-plane database, caller-scoped task/result registry, cancellation tokens and delegation policy. - Closure path: current delegated-task portfolio → model invokes list/check/await → current-control decision → delegate/cancel/continue → lifecycle state changes → updated portfolio. - Boundary reachability: delegate is a first-party runtime tool; background mode returns taskid, and the same surface exposes checkresult, listresults, canceltask and awaitsessions for caller-owned tasks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistence/cancellation mechanics are deterministic, while the model caller decides which current commitments to create, inspect, await or cancel. - Evidence: docs/book/src/agents/delegation.md, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/delegate.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is caller-scoped portfolio control, not a claim that one agent controls every configured task in the installation. - Whole-system current view: within the declared caller-owned delegated-work subsystem, listresults/checkresult expose current lifecycle state and awaitsessions aggregates multiple tasks. - Current-control decision scope: start new delegated work or cancel existing caller-owned background work, changing the current portfolio.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime path establishes an independent complementary auditor whose distinct operational-reality findings close back into corrective S1 behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S3 at the reviewed boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOP audit logging, security/audit records, observability, task recovery and the skill-review fork serve logging/enforcement/recovery or prospective adaptation rather than S3. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: audit loggers store evidence; deterministic monitors enforce/recover state; the model-driven skill reviewer changes future skills and is classified as S4. - Evidence: crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/sop/audit.rs, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/skills/review.rs. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: evidence stores may feed an external/parent audit process, but no such closing runtime actor was established inside the boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: detect durable lessons from completed operational interaction and modify persistent skills that shape future agent behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user corrections, workflow corrections, failed/outdated skills, reusable techniques/workarounds and newly learned task-class knowledge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether a durable adaptation is warranted and directly patch, extend or archive the installed skill library. - Decision owner: the first-party background SKILL REVIEW model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: post-turn trigger threshold, failure-signal extraction, restricted review tools, skill validation, atomic writes, cooldowns and audit metadata. - Closure path: completed operational history/tool outcomes → review fork → model identifies durable adaptation → skillmanage mutates persistent skills → later sessions consume changed skills → future operation changes. - Boundary reachability: the review subsystem ships in zeroclaw-runtime; review.rs identifies the production caller path, and enabling first-party SkillImprovementConfig requires no downstream review implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: trigger/write validation are deterministic, but the model review agent decides whether/what durable lesson to encode and can execute the mutation with approval: None. - Evidence: crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/skills/review.rs, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/skills/reviewprompt.md, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/skillmanage.rs, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/skills/improver.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a configuration-gated shipped operating mode; A does not imply every installation enables it by default. - External distinction: the review fork evaluates evidence from the just-completed operation and user/environment feedback rather than merely continuing the same immediate task loop. - Future / prospective distinction: it decides what durable class-level operating knowledge should govern later sessions, not only how to finish the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: patch an existing skill, add a durable support artifact, create a class-level skill, archive an obsolete skill, or intentionally make no change. - Path back into current capability / S3: accepted review decisions persist directly into the installed skill library; subsequent normal agent turns discover/read those changed skills, so the adapted capability re-enters operational control without a downstream maintainer step.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes organization-level identity/ultimate-policy adjudication closure inside the runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5 at the reviewed boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured identity/personality, risk profiles, supervised approvals, security policy and escalatetohuman constrain/escalate current operation but are not by themselves S5. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a human can answer operational questions and authorize actions, but the reviewed path does not resolve organization identity or constitutional-policy questions as an ultimate-policy function. - Evidence: README.md, crates/zeroclaw-tools/src/escalate.rs, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/identity.rs. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: downstream deployments may place ZeroClaw under a human/external S5 parent; that parent is outside this standalone assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/edbcfbebb4a1d485bbfcc3d232c00f23fd284a21/assessments/zeroclaw.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit edbcfbebb4a1d485bbfcc3d232c00f23fd284a21\">edbcfbe &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T17:46:04+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/greyhaven-ai/autocontext\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">autocontext</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/greyhaven-ai/autocontext/tree/7ccca4681fa113e495c07b0b5b44d28d1181033c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7ccca4681fa113e495c07b0b5b44d28d1181033c\">7ccca46 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform task/scenario attempts and iteratively generate improved strategies/harness artifacts from observed outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: scenario variation, failed actions, changing score and tool/model feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next strategy and propose tools/context mutations after inspecting run feedback. - Decision owner: model-driven competitor/coach/architect roles assembled by the first-party orchestrator, rather than the deterministic runner alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scenario executor, validators, model adapters, tournament, persisted run ledger and time/cost bounds. - Closure path: strategy proposal → scenario actions/trials → measured outcome and analysis → subsequent model proposal or halted iteration. - Boundary reachability: the installed autoctx run/solve CLI invokes AgentOrchestrator.rungeneration through stageagentgeneration in the generation pipeline. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model outputs select nonfixed strategies and next actions; runtime gates constrain publication, not the creative operational choice. - Evidence: pinned orchestrator, stages, CLI and generation pipeline above. - Basis: explicit and structurally wired standard distribution. - Confidence: high for the configured model-backed mode. - Caveats: no external provider call or task-success claim was executed in this static assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate competition and conflicting resource claims between simultaneously executing campaign S1 jobs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers oversubscribing shared concurrency/capacity, duplicate dispatch, stale leases and retry races. - Decisive decision or feedback right: allocate capacity-bounded leases and reservations, reject stale completion, reconcile expired leases, serialize or defer incompatible work. - Decision owner: constructor-defined CampaignScheduler lease/resource algorithm rather than an independently negotiating model coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worker descriptors, global capacity, durable event replay, heartbeat, cancellation and retry state. - Closure path: competing campaign claims → capability/resource reservation and lease assignment → result/timeout/retry recording → subsequent eligible dispatch and changed worker availability. - Boundary reachability: opt-in packaged autoctx campaign run creates the scheduler and drives its lease/dispatch/complete/reconcile paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coordination choices use authored resource rules; they regulate real simultaneous scenario workers, not just route messages between named roles. - Distinct S1 units: independent scenario/agent campaign jobs capable of model-led task actions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous jobs can contend for limited worker slots/capabilities and create duplicate or unaccounted external work after lost leases. - Attenuating coordination relation: the central lease ledger and resource/capacity matching prevent overlapping claims and account for stale completions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: completed/expired/canceled leases and retryable failures update available slots and determine whether/where the next S1 job runs. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the reservation/lease protocol materially suppresses shared-resource interference across distinct running S1 cells. - Evidence: pinned campaignscheduler.py and runtime documentation above. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: medium; optional campaign mode. - Caveats: resource allocation is deterministic and no autonomous S2 arbiter is credited.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate current campaign commitments, scarce resources, continuation and deployment of evaluated harness variants. - Disturbance / variety regulated: over-budget runs, stale worker commitments, unacceptable or inconclusive candidate evidence, and failed promotion checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept/rollback current generation, allocate/cancel campaign work, and keep incumbent versus activate an evaluated candidate. - Decision owner: constructor-defined budget/gate, scheduler and promotion coordinator; model proposal roles do not own the final integrity/activation rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: score/holdout records, candidate digests, run cost trackers, lease ledger, auditor signals and persistence. - Closure path: whole-campaign scores, failures and capacity state → current gate/lease/promotion decision → persisted incumbent/work queue → next generation or dispatch under revised state. - Boundary reachability: the standard generation pipeline invokes gates; optional campaign scheduling and bundle promotion are packaged supported flows with actual coordinator methods. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a proposed strategy alone does not override matched evaluation and promotion policies; final central control remains authored code. - Whole-system current view: active generation results, candidate/incumbent comparisons, campaign lease state, failures, resource limits and budget. - Current-control decision scope: whether present work continues, retries, pauses or rolls back, and whether a validated candidate becomes the current active context. - Evidence: pinned generation pipeline, scheduler and bundle promotion module above. - Basis: structural construction with explicit feedback return. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: no independent managerial actor is inferred from the dashboard or nominal role names.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge ordinary campaign integrity/promotion claims using a differently routed and constrained evidence review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: leaked holdout information, evaluator drift, invalid comparisons, missing reconstruction evidence and unsupported causal assertions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue a cited independent audit finding and severity recommendation on a sealed checkpoint packet. - Decision owner: separately routed model auditor in the explicitly enabled CampaignAuditor mode; deterministic packet guards and policy translation are support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: redacted signed packet builder, distinct endpoint/model-route checks, bounded calls, durable finding store, severity-to-hold policy and operator disposition history. - Closure path: live checkpoint evidence → distinct auditor judgment and cited finding → reviewrequired/safepauserecommended policy outcome → coordinator retains incumbent/holds promotion until disposition or corrective handling. - Boundary reachability: supported AUTOCONTEXTCAMPAIGNAUDITORENABLED with context-bundle promotion or campaign CLI composes the checkpoint runner; ContextBundlePromotionCoordinator tests policy outcomes before activation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unlike ordinary generator scores or the same-role skeptic, a separately configured model backend receives a restricted independent evidence packet and supplies a distinct judgment; it cannot itself rewrite scores or authorize promotion. - Claim being audited: evidence integrity and promotion readiness of candidate context bundles/campaigns. - Ordinary reporting path: tournament and deterministic gates record scores, comparisons, success/failure and candidate promotion eligibility. - Complementary access path: sealed, allowlisted checkpoint metadata and artifact references constructed outside proposer outputs; the audit model reads this under a distinct route. - Independence boundary: the configured auditor endpoint/model identity must differ from all proposer routes absent an explicit unsafe override; it has read-only access, fixed audit checkpoints and durable separate audit records. - Who acts on findings: first-party promotion policy can hold the incumbent; operator or separately configured controller resolves deferred findings. No claim of independent write or final policy authority. - Evidence: pinned campaign auditor, route checks, campaign auditor document and bundle promotion module above. - Basis: explicit optional audit constructor and live corrective gate; not a claim that every default install runs an independent auditor. - Confidence: medium; opt-in mode and policy configuration required. - Caveats: if route distinction is deliberately overridden or the policy is merely advisory, the positive witness does not apply to that mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: model-authored selection of future context/harness changes responsive to changing task/scenario performance rather than only fixing the current one-off answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future task distribution, strategy transfer, scenario performance trends and evolving execution constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: propose new playbook, tool, role-DAG or harness mutations from accumulated scenario outcomes and choose an adaptive candidate for future generations. - Decision owner: model-backed analyst/coach/architect roles; the deterministic bundle validator/promoter governs when a proposed capability can become active. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run history and trajectory, context-bundle store, adaptive matched trials, held-out comparator, mutation parser and promotion gate. - Closure path: external scenario results across generations → model analysis and future-oriented architecture/context proposal → staged candidate evaluation → active bundle change → changed tools/prompts/routing in subsequent operations. - Boundary reachability: stageagentgeneration extracts architect changes and calls proposecontextbundle; the packaged coordinator evaluates/promotes candidates into active scenario context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models select content and architecture options; built-in gates do not themselves create those options. This is restricted to prospective reuse across later generations, not a claim of market or whole-enterprise strategy. - External distinction: held-out and matched scenario task outcomes are environmental evidence beyond the present model response and previous prompt text. - Future / prospective distinction: generated context/role/tool mutations are constructed to change later task attempts rather than merely revise the just-completed answer. - Adaptation option generated: a versioned candidate context bundle containing possible playbook, hint, tool, harness and routing changes. - Path back into current capability / S3: promoted bundle becomes the active context selected by the next run; failed candidates leave the incumbent unchanged. - Evidence: pinned generation stage and context-bundle promotion modules above. - Basis: structural positive path in supported multi-generation model mode. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: evidence is for task-distribution adaptation within the declared harness-evolution boundary, not proactive sensing of an external company strategy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party legitimate authority is shown to decide the overall identity or ultimate priority hierarchy of the harness program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operator-specified purpose, model credentials, permitted tools, resource/risk constraints and audit-policy disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: final identity/ultimate-policy arbitration belongs to the user/operator or owning institution outside this packaged runtime. - Decision owner: external operator; configuration parsing, approvals and auditor dispositions are operational or quality controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: API tokens, allowlists, workspace containment, policy settings, audit-disposition records. - Closure path: an operator configures/approves a bounded run; no shipped constitutional-identity proposal → legitimate parent S5 decision → returned binding identity-policy change is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tools can block unauthorized operations and operators can resolve audit holds, but neither demonstrates that the runtime independently defines its legitimate purpose. - Evidence: pinned CLI documentation, campaign auditor and control modules above. - Basis: negative boundary and ownership test. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a larger deployment may implement a separate S5 parent; not inherited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4e83884ec998546fd68ec77b106b7964cbe589dd/assessments/au/autocontext.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4e83884ec998546fd68ec77b106b7964cbe589dd\">4e83884 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T11:17:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/peakflo/20x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">20x</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/peakflo/20x/tree/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01\">168693b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement real task artifacts through a chosen coding model in a task workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain requirements, source changes, failed commands and tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose actual file edits, tests, tool actions and correction attempts. - Decision owner: model-backed Claude/OpenCode/Codex/Cursor/Pi/ACP task worker, not the desktop scheduler. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent adapter, task prompt, MCP, worktree, transcript, checkpoint and status DB. - Closure path: task brief → model tool calls on repository → changed artifacts and evidence → task ready-for-review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the deterministic Electron app cannot choose code-specific corrections without the external model. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/adapters/coding-agent-adapter.ts - Basis: shipped task-agent execution path; external provider was not run. - Confidence: high for structural decision ownership, not measured outcome quality. - Caveats: human-only tasks and provider engine internals are outside the credited operating mode. - Boundary reachability: standard local task launch with a configured supported agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced specific cross-worker interference repair in shipped task/subtask management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two sibling tasks may duplicate or incompatibly edit a shared source interface. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent model can inspect sibling state but no specific cross-S1 conflict judgment is required or returned. - Decision owner: parent task model owns decomposition; no qualifying inter-S1 coordination decision owner shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child status, worktree isolation, sibling summaries, wait/resume tools. - Closure path: subtask reports flow into parent prompt, without a demonstrated collision-specific correction loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: general instruction to coordinate and shared status do not establish actual inter-S1 variety damping. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/mcp-servers/task-management-core.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/worktree-manager.ts - Basis: bounded source negative across sibling prompt, subtask wait, worktree and parent notification. - Confidence: medium; models could voluntarily reconcile specific conflicts, but not proven as a shipped mode. - Caveats: no claim that sibling workers never coordinate in a user-specific deployment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current task-portfolio assignments and priority/resource fit under a selected model-backed Mastermind/triage mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing current tasks and uneven provider subscription quotas, skill fit and agent workloads. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses suitable agent, priority, skills, repository and children from live agent usage and task evidence. - Decision owner: triage/Mastermind model at current portfolio boundary; operator retains higher authority for risky commands. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listtasks, agent workload statistics, listagents usage limits, updatetask, createsubtask, start/stop tool gates. - Closure path: live portfolio and resource data → model allocation judgment → task DB assignment and priority → subsequent worker launch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fit and priority selection depend on a model comparing work and headroom; tools only execute the choices. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/mcp-servers/task-management-core.ts - Basis: first-party model triage instructions plus writable current task-management API. - Confidence: medium for selected on-demand whole-board mode; not continuous global executive autonomy. - Caveats: single-ticket delegation alone would not suffice; positive mapping uses portfolio-wide task and agent usage information. - Whole-system current view: Mastermind can list all tasks, current statuses, priorities and agent workloads; triage can compare all available agent headroom. - Current-control decision scope: reprioritize or reassign live tasks and pick resource/skill/repository commitments; no S4 strategy or S5 policy power. - Boundary reachability: desktop Mastermind/triage launches with built-in task-management MCP and model-backed session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge task ready-for-review claims against later real PR, CI and linked-issue state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new requested changes, failing CI or external issue drift after the task agent&#39;s last report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate heartbeat model decides whether new external findings are actionable. - Decision owner: fresh heartbeat-specific model session, distinct from original task implementer session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: heartbeat.md, deterministic gh preflight, periodic timer, dedupe and authorization gate. - Closure path: task status/checklist → current external checks → model diagnoses drift → findings forwarded to task agent or to human for authorization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model interprets changed real-world state and requests correction; preflight and forwarding are deterministic controls. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/heartbeat-scheduler.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/shared/system-authority.ts - Basis: implemented separate session/current-state review and returned finding path; no live provider run. - Confidence: medium-high when opt-in monitoring is active. - Caveats: heartbeat requires a useful checklist and enabled task; this is no independent auditor organization. - Claim being audited: task agent&#39;s ready-for-review state implies no unresolved relevant linked PR/CI/issue updates. - Ordinary reporting path: task agent transcript, completion result and status. - Complementary access path: later model session polls current GitHub PR/CI/issue state against authored monitoring checklist. - Independence boundary: independent session and later external access, possibly same underlying provider. - Who acts on findings: task agent receives authorized corrective input; privileged actions are escalated to the human. - Boundary reachability: shipped HeartbeatScheduler auto-enables a new substantive heartbeat.md and runs for eligible ready-for-review tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future external environment option-development loop is coupled to present S3 capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new tools and provider conditions plus changing user needs could require adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user chooses capabilities and model handles immediate task setup, not future strategy options. - Decision owner: human outside system for prospective strategy; no evidenced first-party S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skills sync, user ratings, provider registry and usage statistics. - Closure path: retrospective feedback updates reusable instructions but not a prospective S4/S3 option agreement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: auto-learning and present quota comparisons are not future-facing environmental intelligence. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/session-feedback.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts - Basis: bounded negative across feedback, skills, triage, usage and monitoring. - Confidence: medium-high for selected boundary. - Caveats: external operators can plan strategy independently of 20x.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity-level legitimate ultimate-policy adjudication is implemented within local task workflows. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission checkpoints, risky task steps and user approval conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user approves/rejects ordinary work and configures application settings. - Decision owner: local human owner for task approvals; no evidenced first-party S5 policy tribunal. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: checkpoint tools, heartbeat authority gate, settings and review UI. - Closure path: individual permission may unblock a task but does not establish an identity ruling returned to organizational policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a human action approval is not by itself a governing ultimate-policy exception. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/shared/system-authority.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/mcp-servers/task-management-core.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/session-feedback.ts - Basis: bounded negative across checkpoint, permissions, feedback, configuration and task completion. - Confidence: medium-high within packaged client. - Caveats: owner&#39;s real company governance could provide S5 at another recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d9f102f34f602dcacbc9116de9fcc2e96dabeb4/assessments/20/20x.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d9f102f34f602dcacbc9116de9fcc2e96dabeb4\">2d9f102 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:51:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/orka-agents/orka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orka</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/orka-agents/orka/tree/7cffe130efa74f505d66dce561ade6c18901ba6e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7cffe130efa74f505d66dce561ade6c18901ba6e\">7cffe13 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out user-directed AI tasks, generate code/reviews and use first-party registered tools to produce work artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous prompts, code/test state, model/tool results, errors and task-specific context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an instantiated AI worker or coordinator chooses content-bearing tool calls and task actions based on returned observations. - Decision owner: Orka-instantiated model-driven AI Task/coordinator; external coding CLIs keep their own lower-level agent loops and are not the sole witness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kubernetes Task CRD, pod lifecycle, credentials, workspace, provider proxy, RBAC, task timeout and durable result store. - Closure path: user/Task prompt → worker/model decision → tool or delegated action → pod/run result → subsequent model decision or returned artifact. - Boundary reachability: README&#39;s in-cluster type: ai, chat request handling and registered create/execute tools are supported paths, not only demos. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven selection resolves task content; fixed pod orchestration and auth do not substitute for S1 discretion. - Evidence: README; chat.go; chattoolexecutor.go; general worker. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among sibling specialist Tasks for a configured coordinator mission, including redundant race candidates and result-dependent message handoffs. - Distinct S1 units: separately instantiated coder, reviewer, tester or peer Tasks under one coordinator/transaction, each capable of independently performing work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: redundant simultaneously running alternatives consuming capacity, stale handoffs and sibling actions on incompatible intermediate results. - Attenuating coordination relation: the coordinator may select the first useful race result and cancel other running siblings; configured send/check message and explicit dependency handoff give sibling work a shared decision route instead of independent conflicting continuation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: waitfortasks returns terminal child status/retry metadata, canceltask stops no-longer-useful work and checkmessages changes a receiver&#39;s next step. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is a selected collision/duplication-interference pattern with returned control into separately executing S1s, not generic parallelism or sequential tool calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: race/redundancy and incoherent sibling outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which running child to retain or stop and when an intermediate handoff is safe. - Decision owner: model-driven coordinator only when a coordination-enabled Agent and corresponding task patterns are configured; primitives supply the constructor but no global automatic S2 is assumed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child identity, scoped task authorization, cancellation API, durable messaging and structured terminal waits. - Closure path: concurrent child observations → coordinator judgment about winner/dependency → cancel/message/wait invocation → sibling next behavior/terminal status. - Boundary reachability: coordinated execution and control are shipped as tools, not only example YAML; a configured coordinator can call them via the first-party session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent can make the interference choice in the composed race mode; generic messenger/Task storage alone do not supply a mandatory organization-wide coordinator, hence C rather than A. - Evidence: coordination section, registered delegate, wait, cancel and messages. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current delegated mission, its specialist task status and correction loop, rather than all independent Kubernetes namespaces. - Whole-system current view: the configured coordinator observes the complete active mission&#39;s child-task status, rework, validation and review outcomes, not unaffiliated namespace workloads. - Current-control decision scope: current mission task assignment, retry, rework, cancellation, blocked disposition and completion readiness under the original user&#39;s authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed specialist deliverables, validation/review failures, blocked Tasks and incomplete current mission steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-driven coordinator decides retry/rework/delegation/cancellation from returned child/evaluation feedback and decides when to finalize or report a blocked mission. - Decision owner: configured coordinator agent; platform timeouts and allowlists remain subordinate enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: waitfortasks terminal returns, validation and PR-CI tools, task/agent creation and update, task records, bounded retry budgets in product guidance. - Closure path: mission plan → delegated work → wait/validate/review/CI feedback → coordinator correction/delegation → revised task outputs and terminal mission disposition. - Boundary reachability: createagent coordination and optional initialPrompt initiate a coordinator and subordinate task using the supported API, and the shipped coding workflow guidance explicitly loops over failed validation/reviews before PR/CI closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator interprets heterogeneous current results and chooses corrections, not just fixed scheduling; no all-installation budget/policy S3 is claimed. - Evidence: chatsystemprompt.go; delegatetask.go; waitfortasks.go. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the working PR/task outcome against exact-head evidence, then make findings consequential for mission readiness and rework. - Claim being audited: a coder&#39;s PR head satisfies acceptance, safety and correctness requirements and associated validation passed. - Ordinary reporting path: coder/implementation Task produces changed files, a PR/commit and self-reported success. - Complementary access path: separately configured read-only reviewer Agent receives exact PR head SHA plus bounded changed-file/patch context; the controller rejects stale or malformed verdicts and separately verifies required validation-task provenance. - Independence boundary: dedicated reviewer Task/runtime and read-intent workspace are isolated from the implementation Task, forbidden from modifying the checkout or receiving write credentials. Reviewer model may be an external supported runtime, but Orka owns dispatch, evidence identity, ingestion and return. - Who acts on findings: RepositoryMonitor records needschanges/safety states as blocked readiness; coordinator or explicit orka:fix/orka:fix-ci repair tasks must produce a new exact-head receipt, reset stale review and re-review before readiness. Optional automerge enforces fresh review/CI and policy gates; merge never follows review text alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic self-report, untested diffs, stale review, hidden security/correctness defects, unsupported validation claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer provides an evidence-backed contrary verdict and the monitoring/workflow path can block advancement or require repair. - Decision owner: distinct reviewer Agent for substantive critique; first-party controller for trustworthy exact-head admission and current readiness state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only workspace, signed/scoped inventory, JSON verdict schema, versioned review records, CI and separate validation provenance, bounded repair jobs. - Closure path: submitted PR → exact-head reviewer evidence → accepted verdict/blocked state → corrective agent task or human disposition → new head and re-review → readiness/merge policy. - Boundary reachability: the packaged reviewer Agent and RepositoryMonitor CRD/controller are first-party runnable paths, not a bare example prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critique is a separate model-driven reviewer and its findings are returned through first-party readiness/repair feedback; deterministic SHA/CI checks enforce validity but do not generate the semantic review. - Evidence: RepositoryMonitor, controller, review result ingestion and repair. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct owner evidenced that interprets changing external prospects and selects longer-horizon adaptation of the Orka operating organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/provider changes, future project requirements and workflow evolution were checked as plausible paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no source-backed right to convert trend/forecast/strategy intelligence into a revised ongoing mission capacity/plan, distinct from present PR repair. - Decision owner: users/administrators preconfigure Agents, providers, schedules and policies; individual coding tasks may modify repositories but that is mission S1, not system-level S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scheduled monitors, task templates, runtime-policy config, CI and dashboards. - Closure path: recurring current-state review and operational corrections are evidenced, but not future-looking intelligence → adaptation choice → changed S3 capability → later effectiveness feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: repeated runs, CI repair, model configuration and repository improvement do not alone prove a distinct prospective adaptation owner. - Evidence: README; chatsystemprompt.go; RepositoryMonitor guidance. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no in-boundary unresolved ultimate-identity/policy adjudication and return process is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting goals, namespace trust and provider/tool allowances were considered but are preselected operational constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for an independent identity-policy authority beyond fixed platform administration and task-scoped approvals. - Decision owner: cluster administrators and user/tenant policy configuration; automated admission/RBAC only enforce already chosen constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kubernetes RBAC/admission, namespace authorization, scoped context tokens and provider/model allowlists. - Closure path: operator settings constrain later pods; no open organization identity-policy conflict → legitimate identity decision → authoritative return loop is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: project governance, role prompts, admission policy and security rules do not create a semantic S5 organ. - Evidence: README; security documentation; ToolExecutor authorization. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/10b55d8799d18dedfb18f8aaa4b1c20fd604d200/assessments/or/orka.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 10b55d8799d18dedfb18f8aaa4b1c20fd604d200\">10b55d8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T17:27:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hawkingrei/agenthub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentHub</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hawkingrei/agenthub/tree/7f1f0450ebb58b3cb77b75c9669b4ad1ab8309f7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7f1f0450ebb58b3cb77b75c9669b4ad1ab8309f7\">7f1f045 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Workers deliver assigned engineering outcomes with local initiative and repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementation/test failures, new findings and scoped blockers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select local investigation/actions/repair and report evidence rather than wait for coordinator micro-approval. - Decision owner: Worker agent under the shipped worker execution role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP provider bridge, role-skill installation, task records, workspace tools and evidence stores. - Closure path: Task assignment/context reaches worker → agent acts → workerstatus/artifact pointers reach coordinator → accepted review or concrete change request drives continued work. - Boundary reachability: Team creation selects compiled worker prompt and role skill profile; ACP attaches mandatory managed skills before execution. External CLI is execution support, not an unexamined donor of the role’s rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Worker prompt explicitly gives initiative within its lane. Removing the agent leaves transport/task state without the same implementation decisions. - Evidence: skills/team/team-worker-executor.SKILL.md; crates/agenthub-team-prompts/prompts/defaultteamworkerprompt.txt; crates/agenthub-acp/src/teamroleskills.rs; src/api/teams.rs - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent competing agents from simultaneously owning the same actionable mailbox topic and reconcile ownership/dependency conflicts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple workers can claim one topic or conflicting assignments; claim rejection identifies the active owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent chooses claim, watch/wait, release or peer/coordinator adjustment after concrete ownership feedback. - Decision owner: Participating Team agents; coordinator resolves cross-worker ambiguity when needed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite topic claim/lease transaction, stable topic keys, mailbox error/status feedback and shipped mailbox discipline. - Closure path: A live conflicting claim returns ThreadClaimConflict/owner; losing agent switches to watch/wait rather than executing as a second owner; release/expiry enables later accepted work. - Boundary reachability: Both worker/coordinator ACP roles require the actor-mailbox skill. The shipped actor triage path invokes the same claim transaction and exposes its concrete conflict recovery; custom coordination code is not required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SQL exclusion only enforces the selected claim. The mandatory agent mailbox contract supplies the behavior-changing triage response; a bare mailbox would not suffice. - Evidence: skills/team/team-actor-mailbox.SKILL.md; src/team/manager/mailboxthreads.rs; src/team/manager/mailboxerrors.rs; crates/agenthub-acp/src/teamroleskills.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Source-based boundary judgment; no live model or production effectiveness claim. - Distinct S1 units: separate worker agents with assigned outcomes and local implementation discretion in the same Team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing active ownership of one topic/task-related thread, not merely two agents exchanging messages. - Attenuating coordination relation: claim ownership plus agent watch/wait/release and explicit handoff. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflict response names owner and instructs loser to watch/release/wait, withholding duplicate execution until ownership permits it. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: source implements and the installed skill handles an actual exclusive-ownership collision, beyond message delivery.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Maintain cohesion of current Team work through explicit ownership, priority, accountability and intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale/duplicated tasks, blocked workers, ownership mismatch, competing priorities and unsupported completion claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose/revise canonical assignee/priority and current task commitments, park blocked work, accept/reopen based on evidence. - Decision owner: Coordinator agent in the installed Team mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Actor task create/list/update tools, task journals, execution context, mailbox evidence, role/permission gates and runtime views. - Closure path: Coordinator reads current task/member evidence → prioritizes or reassigns/changes state with journal reason → persisted canonical task/actor message changes worker’s next lane → returned evidence is reconciled again. - Boundary reachability: Compiled coordinator prompt and mandatory role skills explicitly route to task governance/lifecycle; supported actor/API tools persist chosen priorities and assignees. No adjacent contributor-management agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This extends beyond decomposing one prompt: the role deliberately reconciles ongoing task truth, stale duplicates, ownership drift, waiting commitments and evidence freshness. Runtime SQL/permissions execute rather than choose these decisions. - Evidence: skills/team/team-coordinator-orchestrator.SKILL.md; skills/team/team-task-governance.SKILL.md; skills/team/team-task-lifecycle.SKILL.md; src/team/manager/taskcatalog.rs; src/team/manager/taskupdates.rs; src/api/teams.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Source-based boundary judgment; no live model or production effectiveness claim. - Whole-system current view: Team task set/Kanban, member identity cards and checkpoints, blockers, notes and mailbox evidence. - Current-control decision scope: owner/priority/replanning, task commitment/acceptance and block/unblock intervention across the current Team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No additional materially independent complementary operational-audit path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading execution or management claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately authoritative audit judgment and corrective control return. - Decision owner: Coordinator reviews ordinary worker evidence; permission reviewer decides narrower execution permission. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP event history, task review, permissions and developer prompt-review plugin. - Closure path: Task inreview returns requested changes to worker, but this is the normal delivery/acceptance path; no separate complementary organizational-audit mode is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Architect/reviewer naming and raw logs alone do not turn normal coordinator acceptance into S3. - Evidence: skills/team/team-task-lifecycle.SKILL.md; src/team/permissionreview/dispatcher.rs; src/team/permissionreview/selection.rs; plugins/agenthub-prompt-engineering/skills/agenthub-team-prompt-review/SKILL.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective environmental adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future capability/environment change beyond current task learning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Develop/admit prospective organizational capability options from external distinctions. - Decision owner: Agents retain project lessons and may patch their own profile; user controls broader product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace memory, profilepatchproposal, timed follow-ups and runtime recovery spine. - Closure path: Selected notes/profile changes inform later tasks, without an established external/future option-to-capability regulatory conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Self-profile drift repair and durable lessons do not alone establish S4; planned Mem/activation design cannot supply current closure. - Evidence: docs/features/team-workspace-memory-contract.md; src/api/teams.rs; skills/team/team-worker-executor.SKILL.md; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate-policy/identity adjudication loop at Team recursion established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Purpose/ultimate-policy tension, beyond member identity metadata and execution risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Return an authoritative ultimate-purpose/policy decision into subsequent Team operation. - Decision owner: User configures Team; agents own only scoped self-profile proposals and operational commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent cards, role skill restrictions, profile patches and permission approvals. - Closure path: Description/prompt changes update local role metadata; ACP approvals permit scoped commands. Neither is an evidenced ultimate-policy decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The word identity in Agent Cards does not make role-metadata editing S5. - Evidence: src/api/teams.rs; skills/team/TEAMAGENTS.md; skills/team/team-task-governance.SKILL.md; src/team/permissionreview/dispatcher.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/16805b7f353f8cb58b1aafb55c1c3f20ef01076a/assessments/ag/agenthub.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 16805b7f353f8cb58b1aafb55c1c3f20ef01076a\">16805b7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T22:11:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Chorus-AIDLC/Chorus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chorus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Chorus-AIDLC/Chorus/tree/148b7928b99773f7fbdf61080940bd6995b06934\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 148b7928b99773f7fbdf61080940bd6995b06934\">148b792 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement bounded Chorus tasks and produce software changes/evidence under the task&#39;s acceptance criteria and project context. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, implementation choices, failures, tests, project constraints and task-specific ambiguity encountered while completing assigned work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool/debugging actions needed to satisfy an assigned task and decide when work is ready to submit for verification. - Decision owner: the autonomous task worker agent launched under the shipped Chorus develop/worker protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task/proposal state, acceptance criteria, permissions, session/daemon dispatch, agent integration adapters, Chorus MCP tools and persisted work reports. - Closure path: orchestrator dispatches an unblocked task → worker reads Chorus context and executes autonomously in the target repository → worker reports work/self-check and submits for verification → resulting state/evidence returns to Chorus for audit/control. - Boundary reachability: Chorus ships the worker/develop skill contracts and supported adapters/daemon paths that launch autonomous coding-agent sessions as the standard operating surface; the external coding harness supplies execution capabilities but the Chorus project/task contract and return path are first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the task contract constrains scope, while the running worker agent chooses the substantive implementation sequence and tool actions rather than Chorus deterministically prescribing them. - Evidence: README.md, $yolo task execution. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S1 autonomy is credited to the running task-agent role reachable through Chorus&#39;s first-party integration surfaces, not to deterministic task-state machinery or to undocumented behavior of any particular external coding harness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific regulation of a demonstrated interference/oscillation among distinct S1 workers is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an S2 disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task DAG dependencies, unblocked-task queries, dependency-ordered waves, project/task state, comments and worker isolation organize and sequence work but do not by themselves establish an inter-S1 conflict/oscillation witness. - Closure path: dependency completion changes which tasks become runnable, but this is task sequencing/current control rather than evidence of a separate S2 attenuation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple workers and a DAG are insufficient under the Profile; the reviewed evidence does not identify a concrete recurring interference between S1 units and a distinct coordination relation targeted at attenuating that interference. - Evidence: $yolo wave execution, README.md. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dependency ordering can prevent invalid execution order, but no separate S2-specific interference/attenuation relation is evidenced strongly enough to publish a positive state.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a project-wide current operational view and decide which commitments start, wait, reopen, advance or escalate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete dependencies, failed or missing worker submissions, task-review failures, proposal-review failures, stuck work, exhausted review rounds and changing readiness across the task DAG. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next dependency-ready task wave, dispatch workers, decide how to handle non-ready/failed results, reopen failed tasks, advance verified tasks and stop/escalate when the project cannot progress. - Decision owner: the model-driven orchestrating agent executing the first-party $yolo workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent project/proposal/task state, chorusgetunblockedtasks, status transitions, acceptance criteria, admin transition APIs, reviewer comments, permissions and daemon/session lifecycle. - Closure path: orchestrator reads current project/task/review state → chooses wave/advance/reopen/escalate action → Chorus state/API transitions and worker dispatch implement it → changed task states alter subsequent readiness and execution → refreshed project state returns to the orchestrator. - Boundary reachability: $yolo is a shipped first-party Chorus skill and explicitly provides a complete autonomous mode using Chorus MCP/project state; its current-control decisions are not borrowed from repository CI or human project administration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-state and dependency code enforce constraints, but the orchestrating agent chooses the current portfolio action, handles exceptional results and decides whether to continue, rework or escalate. - Whole-system current view: project, proposal, task DAG, unblocked tasks, task statuses, review comments/verdicts and completion state across the Chorus project. - Current-control decision scope: current worker commitments, wave dispatch, rework/reopen, progression after review, and escalation when progress conditions cannot be satisfied. - Evidence: plugins/chorus/skills/yolo/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: server-side validation, permission checks and dependency computation are enforcement/support. S3 credit comes from the orchestrating agent&#39;s evidenced project-wide current-control choices.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge proposal and implementation completion claims through separate read-only reviewer agents and return blocking findings into corrective project operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing requirements, weak acceptance criteria, dependency/design errors, implementation drift, circular or inadequate self-tests, build/test failure and developer self-verification blind spots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect evidence independently, classify findings as blocking/non-blocking and issue the current round&#39;s VERDICT that determines whether the orchestrator advances or returns the work for correction. - Decision owner: the separate proposal-reviewer or task-reviewer agent for the corresponding audit stage. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only reviewer permissions, proposal/task/Idea/comment APIs, filesystem/test/build inspection, structured verdict format, persisted comments, review-round limits and admin state-transition APIs. - Closure path: producer submits proposal/task → distinct reviewer gathers complementary evidence and posts current-round verdict → orchestrator reads that verdict → FAIL causes reject/revise/resubmit or reopen/rework → the corrected artifact is reviewed again before advancement. - Boundary reachability: both reviewer definitions and the orchestration that invokes/consumes them are shipped first-party Chorus plugin surfaces in the standard autonomous workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewers make substantive adversarial judgments from separate evidence and are prevented from editing the work under review; deterministic state transitions only enforce the downstream response selected from the verdict. - Claim being audited: proposal completeness/alignment before implementation and task implementation/acceptance correctness before verification/completion. - Ordinary reporting path: PM/developer agents create proposal drafts or implementation, self-check/report work and submit it through ordinary Chorus state transitions. - Complementary access path: reviewer agents separately fetch originating Idea/proposal/task/AC/comments and inspect project files, diffs, tests/builds or proposal dependency coverage under read-only constraints. - Independence boundary: reviewer roles are separate spawned agents with explicit read-only restrictions and distinct reviewer contracts; they do not author the proposal/code they evaluate during the review pass. - Who acts on findings: the $yolo orchestrator reads the current verdict and rejects/reopens or approves/verifies accordingly, causing the affected planning/development agents to perform subsequent correction. - Evidence: proposal reviewer, task reviewer, $yolo review loops. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: reviewer verdict strings are not credited merely because they are called review; the positive state rests on separate evidence access, read-only independence and an explicit findings-to-rework closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then adaptation loop is established at the Chorus-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S4 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project search, Idea elaboration, proposal generation, repository/context inspection, persistent reports and reviewer feedback improve current project execution but do not establish prospective environment sensing that changes the harness/project&#39;s future capability program. - Closure path: current-project information feeds current planning and rework; no separate future-facing adaptation selection/return loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future tasks inside the current product request and learning from current review are not sufficient to map S4 without an environment-facing prospective adaptation loop. - Evidence: $yolo planning/review workflow, README.md. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Chorus can be extended and its agents can research externally, but generic agent capability or project planning does not establish S4 ownership for the harness at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the Chorus-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user prompt/Idea intent, API-key permissions, admin approval/verification rights, project settings and review-round limits constrain operation but are operational/task policy rather than an evidenced S5 identity loop. - Closure path: permissions and approvals affect allowed current actions, but no disputed identity/ultimate-policy matter is routed to an authoritative S5 actor and returned as governing identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user origin, admin permission or approval gate is not automatically S5; the reviewed project boundary does not expose an identity-level policy negotiation/closure path. - Evidence: README.md, $yolo prerequisite/admin flow. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: humans can operate/administer Chorus and configure permissions, but those facts alone do not establish a parent-governed S5 function under Methodology 0.3.5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/60fa7f6cbad5d00ad76aac4e43ee85735aaa106f/assessments/chorus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 60fa7f6cbad5d00ad76aac4e43ee85735aaa106f\">60fa7f6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T21:13:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sd0xdev/sd0x-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sd0x-dev-flow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sd0xdev/sd0x-harness/tree/04e8a5e3f5d9c482937c2ad2170fab5fbd02b630\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 04e8a5e3f5d9c482937c2ad2170fab5fbd02b630\">04e8a5e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement and verify a requested software change or investigation in a user project through model-directed project work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar project context, failing tests, faulty edits, changed requirements and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose inspections, edits, tests, delegated specialist assistance and repairs in response to evidence. - Decision owner: model-driven Claude Code engineering actor using the installed first-party project rules and specialist skills; the external Claude host executes tool calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin rules, installed project setup, hooks, review-state digest, git guards and verification scripts. - Closure path: user task → actor work and tools → test/review evidence or failure → actor correction/re-verification → project changes and report. - Boundary reachability: the documented full Claude plugin mode installs skills/rules/hooks and the session imports them; unlike skills-only mode, the full mode supplies the completion and review path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the actor selects engineering actions, whereas scripts record and gate outcomes without choosing code changes; external host internals are not credited as first-party decisions. - Evidence: installation and ownership model, active session rules, project setup and installed surfaces, hook wiring. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: source demonstrates a supported installed mode, not deployment frequency or measured task success; first-party credit is for the composed plugin mode, not the external host&#39;s general autonomous capabilities.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 interference regulation is established for the assessed standard coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: read-only fanout and specialist handoffs are present, but no concrete conflicting changes between autonomous S1 work cells are regulated by a specified returned coordination decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: not established; planner/executor role dispatch and report ordering do not themselves decide collision attenuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: report-only workflow planning, bounded read-only fanout and sequential specialist skill calls. - Closure path: workflow reports return to the caller; no evidenced change to later competing S1 behaviour from conflict regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven orchestration of independent read-only findings alone is not S2. - Evidence: read-only orchestrate contract, single reviewer default, specialist definition. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: other user-composed multi-agent modes are possible but are not credited without a shipped interference-specific return loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control relation is established at the configured project-session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk severity, code/doc dirtiness and review failures are constrained by local quality gates, not shown as current resource bargaining across operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: risk tier and gate obligations operate on task/change evidence without an evidenced whole-system allocation or corrective control decision. - Decision owner: model and user choose execution/review details within static policy; digest-state scripts enforce/report. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Anchor Register, tier selection, review slots, hook reminders, scoped guards and human exits. - Closure path: review failures cause local work/review retries; no independently evidenced S3 current-control feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent-owned local repair and deterministic completion invariant do not themselves establish S3. - Evidence: review tiers and loop, policy tiers and ownership, digest-bound state, nonblocking hook. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: risk-scaled verification is important but cannot substitute for a whole-system current view.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an implementer&#39;s completion/correctness claim by inspecting actual changed project artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a producer may falsely declare completion or miss faults not visible in its own summary/checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: give a separate severity-scoped audit verdict from raw code/diff access and determine whether corrective work is owed. - Decision owner: the separately dispatched Codex reviewer model makes the audit judgment; the producing Claude actor owns subsequent repairs; no digest parser is credited as auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only codex exec transport, frozen changed-file metadata, review-state verdict slot, risk thresholds and fallback validation. - Closure path: after code changes, /codex-review-fast → independent reviewer source investigation → Ready/Blocked findings → producing actor fixes blocking issues → re-review → fresh pass records a digest-bound slot required before completion. - Boundary reachability: the full plugin installs the review skill, transport and model-facing auto-loop obligations, and hooks remind after edits; the review is a documented first-party supported optional tool path, not a CI-only or developer-dogfood audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a different model agent reads the actual diff/source and decides findings; transport, severity parser and receipt record do not substitute for its judgment. - Claim being audited: project change is correct enough under the configured risk tier to be complete. - Ordinary reporting path: producer&#39;s change summary, tests and its own verification. - Complementary access path: separate Codex sandbox source/diff inspection and independent review prompt; fallback reviewers are controlled by recorded transport failure semantics. - Independence boundary: read-only external Codex session receives frozen task and changed-file metadata but reads source itself; it is not the producer&#39;s self-verification. Both hosted tools remain outside first-party implementation ownership. - Who acts on findings: producing Claude actor performs scoped fixes and re-dispatches; human is reserved for explicitly enumerated exits. - Evidence: independent source review and fix loop, severity/scope gate, separate reviewer transport, repeat-until-current-pass contract, digest-bound return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: evidence is shipped protocol/code, not a captured successful live independent audit; review may be blocked by missing external Codex credentials or human exits in particular installations.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: adapt subsequent project-engineering guidance from recurring external/user corrections. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated user-observed failures whose recurrence is not prevented by current rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinguish a reusable error pattern and, after repetition, update project-local operational guidance for future sessions. - Decision owner: the model-driven engineering actor following the first-party self-improvement rule; the user can originate corrections and decide version-control inclusion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: namespaced lesson log, repeated-pattern criterion, rule-file update and normal doc review. - Closure path: user correction on a recurrent failure → actor writes a structured lesson with prevention signal → pattern reaches promotion criterion → actor promotes guidance to project rules → future sessions read updated rules and act differently. - Boundary reachability: the published self-improvement rule is installed via standard full-plugin project setup and imported by session guidance; the namespaced lesson file is provisioned on first occurrence and later local rules are loaded. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model must recognize the pattern, select the prevention and write/publish the operational rule; file persistence alone is not the adaptation judgment. - External distinction: user correction provides external feedback about unmet project expectations, not solely internal planning. - Future / prospective distinction: lesson/rule promotion addresses future recurrence across tasks and sessions. - Adaptation option generated: prevention rule and detection signal reusable for later work. - Path back into current capability / S3: project-installed rules govern later Claude sessions, closing adaptation into operational capability; no distinct S3 is claimed. - Evidence: correction-to-rule procedure, rule installation, project rule imports. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: contingent on actual recurring correction and session-level rule promotion; source does not establish measured improvement or autonomous change to the plugin&#39;s published universal anchors.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return loop is established in the supported project engineering session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local configuration, security anchors, scope requests and approval/merge risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no in-boundary identity-level decision is returned through an established S5 channel. - Decision owner: developer and user set framework identity and non-negotiable rules; model may deviate from defaults only inside those constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed Anchor Register, project overrides, user confirmation and protected git/review gates. - Closure path: local tasks use preselected rules; no evidenced policy-level proposal → legitimate parent judgment → returned change to the session&#39;s identity/ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static constraint hierarchy and user approval of ordinary actions do not create S5 closure. - Evidence: Anchor/Default boundaries, fixed security policy, scope and developer role. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: project maintainers can change the published plugin policy in a different recursion, which is explicitly excluded here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d6959de776c5df5b41ae7fb77c94cfd9bd6236ee/assessments/sd/sd0x-dev-flow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d6959de776c5df5b41ae7fb77c94cfd9bd6236ee\">d6959de &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T21:37:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/your-claude-engineer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Your Claude Engineer</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/your-claude-engineer/tree/e7c064ac915cfeb29496c558795efa125a121863\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e7c064ac915cfeb29496c558795efa125a121863\">e7c064a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement a selected app feature through model-selected coding actions, browser tests and correction from tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete feature requirements, changing code context, failing browser flows, runtime errors and interrupted long-running model sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coding model chooses concrete edits, commands, browser inspections and repairs from the orchestrator&#39;s issue context and observed tool outcomes. - Decision owner: model-backed coding subagent executed through Claude Agent SDK; the first-party harness supplies role/prompt/tool configuration and continuity, not the SDK model&#39;s entire general capability. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generated project directory, first-party subagent definitions, Arcade tools, sandbox, bash pretool hook and retry/session boundaries. - Closure path: app specification and Linear issue → orchestrator delegates detailed work → coding model inspects/edits/tests → Playwright or tool result → fix and verification → implementation evidence returned to orchestrator/Linear. - Boundary reachability: autonomousagentdemo.py invokes the first-party run loop; client.py wires AGENTDEFINITIONS and built-in/Playwright tools into the SDK client; coding prompt directs real code/test actions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: implementation and repair actions depend on model interpretation of evidence; the shell allowlist, iteration delay and state markers cannot make those decisions alone. - Evidence: entry/session loop, SDK client and tool wiring, specialized coding definition, coding prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a live provider/Arcade/Playwright session was not run; SDK authentication and third-party success remain outside the evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct mechanism is shown for attenuating a concrete disturbance between independently viable, competing operational S1 coding units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear, coding, GitHub and Slack agents exchange messages and roles, but no competing source-changing S1 claims or resource-contention disturbance is evidenced in the default sequential one-issue flow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific allocation, negotiated conflict-resolution or revised reciprocal S1 commitments. - Decision owner: none shown; orchestrator transfers context and orders specialized tasks, while SDK Task dispatch executes bounded child work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role separation, Linear statuses, one-issue sequencing, local generated-project git and task handoff summaries. - Closure path: orchestrator orders a single implementation issue and passes results to Linear/GitHub/Slack; this is cross-role task routing, not a source-backed interference-damping return between contending operational cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model-chosen next issue belongs to whole-current S3 at this recursion; it does not itself close S2 competition among independent parallel work units. - Evidence: explicit one-issue-at-a-time rule and context transfer, sequential feature workflow, role definitions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external SDK may support parallel Task calls, but this source does not demonstrate a distinct concrete S2 attenuation path for the shipped project workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current whole project backlog, work priority and resumed progress across many feature issues/sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stale issue status, wrong next-work choice, incomplete previously Done features, session context resets and missing acceptance evidence. - Whole-system current view: the model orchestrator requests Linear META context, counts across Done/In Progress/Todo and full details of the next issue, and reads the generated project&#39;s current state before deciding its continuation. - Current-control decision scope: model selects whether to stop and repair regression or proceed to the next feature, delegates the issue and decides when to mark it Done based on required browser evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which pending portfolio issue should be worked next and overturn current progress when verification fails; issue transitions/continuation return to Linear and next fresh session. - Decision owner: first-party prompted model orchestrator with live Linear agent and coding-agent feedback; neither static CLI loop nor Linear SaaS is credited with the model&#39;s project-wide decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: META issue, .linearproject.json continuation marker, project progress summary, subagent tools, status counts, completion signal. - Closure path: fresh session reads project/Linear whole-current status → orchestrator asks for retest → if failed directs correction, else chooses next issue → dispatches code and evidence → updates Linear Done/next state → next session refreshes the picture. - Boundary reachability: the active client loads orchestratorprompt.md and the iteration loop recreates a client per session, so this is not an unused design diagram. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the prompt gives the model discretion to choose/reprioritize actual current work and act on regression findings; the deterministic loop only reopens sessions and recognizes the completion token. - Evidence: orchestrator continuation and priority/verification steps, whole-project completion decision, fresh-session state and rerun, live prompt registration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the whole here is the one generated app&#39;s issue portfolio, not all of an enterprise&#39;s projects; the Linear account&#39;s actual data/permissions were not accessed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: test prior completed-feature claims against actual running UI/interaction evidence before the S3 orchestrator takes new work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear may say Done while a previously working feature has regressed; summary or screenshot receipts can be stale or misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven coding subagent runs a fresh browser verification for 1–2 completed features and can return FAIL plus screenshots; orchestrator must suspend new work and request regression fixing. - Decision owner: separately invoked coding verifier model for direct Playwright observations; orchestrator owns current feature plan and the fix order. This is the same coding role as producers, but a distinct subsequent Task invocation, not a third-party auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mandatory pre-work verification gate, browser navigation/click/snapshot/screenshot tools, structured PASS/FAIL report, no-Done-without-screenshot rule. - Closure path: prior Linear Done claim → new continuation requests browser retest → independent current app UI observations → FAIL evidence → orchestrator orders fix and re-test before new task → updated issue/current project status. - Boundary reachability: orchestrator prompt makes retest mandatory on every continuation/new feature; coding subagent prompt defines a dedicated Verification Test task; Playwright tools are registered in the normal SDK client. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model verifier decides whether the live UI corroborates the prior completion account; neither the prior producer&#39;s summary nor a deterministic status parser supplies this judgment. - Claim being audited: previously marked-Done project features still work and are fit to leave closed. - Ordinary reporting path: Linear Done status, prior implementation/test summary and saved project/meta issue context. - Complementary access path: fresh coding verifier goes to running app via Playwright, exercises 1–2 completed features, observes screenshots/browser state and reports PASS/FAIL. - Independence boundary: verifier is a fresh delegated Task/subagent context relative to prior iteration and S3 issue status, but reuses the &#39;coding&#39; role/provider and is not organizationally independent; that narrower evidence-based independence is the claim credited. - Who acts on findings: S3 orchestrator blocks next feature and asks coding agent to repair a reproduced regression; verification repeats before onward progress. - Evidence: mandatory verification and remedial gate, continuation&#39;s direct retest and failure return, coding agent browser verification protocol, tool registry. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: prompts specify behavior; no captured live regression was reproduced in this review. The same coding agent role is reused, so independence is bounded to fresh contextual sensing versus S3/earlier Done account, not external independent peer review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped organizational loop selects durable prospective changes to the harness&#39;s future capabilities from external environmental intelligence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user-specified app goals and task regressions are operations/current control, not an ongoing externally informed future adaptation mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none beyond issue-by-issue project implementation and user-edited prompts/spec/config. - Decision owner: user supplies app specification and permission settings; orchestrator chooses present feature priority but does not autonomously revise the harness&#39;s strategic operating design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: appspec.txt, developer-editable prompt files, Linear backlog, META continuation memory and model configuration flags. - Closure path: spec/backlog → present coding and feature selection; no external change signal → strategic option → selected lasting future operating-capability shift is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: long-running context and future work items are ordinary S1/S3 continuity, not independent S4 environmental search/adaptation. - External distinction: third-party project requirements and Linear status are task inputs, not autonomous environmental foresight. - Future / prospective distinction: an unfinished backlog is a plan for task execution, not adaptation of the executing organization&#39;s future structure. - Evidence: project specification and prompt customization, current issue workflow, session continuity. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user can change the prompts or request a different application; that is external reconfiguration, not a built-in strategic return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party autonomous ultimate-policy or legitimate organizational identity owner closes an S5 constitutional return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permission, command safety, OAuth authorization and product scope are externally pre-set restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for determining the project&#39;s ultimate legitimate purpose or amendments to binding identity/policy. - Decision owner: project owner, external credentials administrator and fixed script/security policies govern these matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude SDK sandbox, file tool allowlist, bash PreToolUse hook, Arcade OAuth, fixed appspec and allowed-command list. - Closure path: configuration constrains tool execution; no first-party deliberation over constitutional goals and downward reauthorization is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: enforcement of static user/configuration rights is not policy-identity formation. - Evidence: security and permission construction, SDK tool/hook invocation, first-party bash guard. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within reviewed boundary. - Caveats: external user/organization policy is excluded from this system in focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/21315010a2fe15de7f6fd1c8c001cbedcbb865f7/assessments/yo/your-claude-engineer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 21315010a2fe15de7f6fd1c8c001cbedcbb865f7\">2131501 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
